window.PREP_DATA = {"config": {"title": "Hatch Interview Flashcards", "brand": "Hatch Prep", "interview": {"label": "Thu Nov 19, 11:00", "datetime": "2026-11-19T11:00:00-05:00"}}, "sections": {"H": "Hatch interview", "E": "Experience", "C": "Capstone", "R": "Research project", "L": "Luminance-GS original", "M": "Metrics", "F": "CV fundamentals", "I": "Industrial vision", "N": "ML fundamentals"}, "cards": [{"id": "H01", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Tell me about yourself", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["Who", "Capstone", "Research", "Industry + why here"], "hint2": [["Who", "Computer Engineering", "Dec 2026", "Engineering Physics"], ["Capstone", "Tim Hortons cups", "mixed recycling", "moving conveyor", "vacuum gantry", "YOLOv3", "95%"], ["Research", "3D reconstruction", "255k-parameter", "~70 parameters", "+2.1 dB"], ["Industry + why here", "L3Harris", "Hitachi Rail", "vision on real equipment, tested on site"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Who:<\/strong> MEng <strong>Computer Engineering<\/strong>, McMaster, finishing <strong>Dec 2026<\/strong>; BEng <strong>Engineering Physics<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Capstone:<\/strong> a robot that finds <strong>Tim Hortons cups<\/strong> in <strong>mixed recycling<\/strong> on a <strong>moving conveyor<\/strong> and lifts them out with a <strong>vacuum gantry<\/strong>: <strong>YOLOv3<\/strong>, <strong>95%<\/strong> removed.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Research:<\/strong> low-light <strong>3D reconstruction<\/strong>; replaced a <strong>255k-parameter<\/strong> module with <strong>~70 parameters<\/strong>; <strong>+2.1 dB<\/strong> when exposures vary.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Industry + why here:<\/strong> <strong>L3Harris<\/strong> laser calibration tool, <strong>Hitachi Rail<\/strong> AI team lead; I want <strong>vision on real equipment, tested on site<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> who I am, the robot, the research, industry, why this job. Under 75 seconds, then stop.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H02", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Why Hatch? Why this role?", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Honest hook", "Fit", "Motivation"], "hint2": [["Honest hook", "big mining, metals and energy projects", "AAR", "vision and AI on robots in real plants"], ["Fit", "vision-guided robot", "imaging in bad light", "dust, heat, glare and low light"], ["Motivation", "physical AI", "acts in the real world", "a better world through positive change", "safer, cleaner", "mentorship", "P.Eng"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Honest hook:<\/strong> I knew Hatch for <strong>big mining, metals and energy projects<\/strong>. This posting showed me <strong>AAR<\/strong>: <strong>vision and AI on robots in real plants<\/strong>, which I didn’t know you did.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Fit:<\/strong> it’s what I’ve already built: a <strong>vision-guided robot<\/strong> (capstone) and <strong>imaging in bad light<\/strong> (research). Mining is <strong>dust, heat, glare and low light<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Motivation:<\/strong> I’m fascinated by <strong>physical AI<\/strong>, AI that <strong>acts in the real world<\/strong>. Your aim of “<strong>a better world through positive change<\/strong>” here means <strong>safer, cleaner<\/strong> operations; plus <strong>mentorship<\/strong> and a path to <strong>P.Eng<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Don’t say you “never cared” about Hatch before. Say what surprised you.<\/em><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H03", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "What do you know about Hatch?", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["70+ years", "AAR", "Industrial robotics", "Recent news"], "hint2": [["70+ years", "10,000 colleagues", "150 countries", "Metals, Energy, Infrastructure", "a better world through positive change", "entrepreneurs with a technical soul"], ["AAR", "Technologies", "Business Practices: Equipment Technology"], ["Industrial robotics", "heat, dust, corrosion, impact", "2D vision, 3D structured light, laser triangulation", "“on the fly”", "telerobotics"], ["Recent news", "AI-Assisted Process Control", "under 3 months", "LayerSense"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>70+ years<\/strong>, <strong>10,000 colleagues<\/strong>, <strong>150 countries<\/strong>; sectors <strong>Metals, Energy, Infrastructure<\/strong>. Aim: “<strong>a better world through positive change<\/strong>”; motto: “<strong>entrepreneurs with a technical soul<\/strong>”.<\/li>\n<li><strong>AAR<\/strong> sits in the <strong>Technologies<\/strong> team under <strong>Business Practices: Equipment Technology<\/strong>, Mississauga, supporting offices worldwide.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Industrial robotics:<\/strong> robots hardened for <strong>heat, dust, corrosion, impact<\/strong>; <strong>2D vision, 3D structured light, laser triangulation<\/strong>; motion synced <strong>“on the fly”<\/strong>; <strong>telerobotics<\/strong> with haptic feedback.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Recent news:<\/strong> <strong>AI-Assisted Process Control<\/strong> at MMG Dugald River (payback <strong>under 3 months<\/strong>, July 2026); <strong>LayerSense<\/strong> ultrasonic inspection of furnaces and pipelines (Aug 2026).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H04", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Where do you see yourself in 5 years?", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["senior AI / computer vision engineer", "proof-of-concept to commissioning"], "hint2": [["senior AI / computer vision engineer", "leads a team", "leading edge of vision", "smarter and safer"], ["proof-of-concept to commissioning", "P.Eng", "mentoring"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A <strong>senior AI / computer vision engineer<\/strong> who <strong>leads a team<\/strong> and works at the <strong>leading edge of vision<\/strong>, making these machines <strong>smarter and safer<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Having taken systems from <strong>proof-of-concept to commissioning<\/strong> on client sites, with my <strong>P.Eng<\/strong>, and <strong>mentoring<\/strong> new trainees.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> lead, stay at the edge of the tech, still hands-on with real machines.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H05", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "What is your greatest weakness?", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Weakness", "What I do now"], "hint2": [["Weakness", "exciting part first", "multi-agent system", "unglamorous groundwork", "baselines, A/B tests, regression tests"], ["What I do now", "fundamentals first", "40-defect benchmark", "37% → 82%", "reproduce the baseline and write pass/fail rules before any experiment"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Weakness:<\/strong> I’m drawn to the <strong>exciting part first<\/strong> (e.g. designing the <strong>multi-agent system<\/strong> at Hitachi) before the <strong>unglamorous groundwork<\/strong>: <strong>baselines, A/B tests, regression tests<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>What I do now:<\/strong> <strong>fundamentals first<\/strong>. At Hitachi I built a <strong>40-defect benchmark<\/strong> before tuning the agents, which is how we could show <strong>37% → 82%<\/strong>. In research I <strong>reproduce the baseline and write pass/fail rules before any experiment<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H06", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Learned something new quickly", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "vision system", "ChatGPT launched that semester", "no AI to lean on"], ["T", "drives a robot", "April"], ["A", "docs, tutorials and trial and error", "YOLO, OpenCV", "Colab", "control side", "ESP32", "stepper maths", "serial link"], ["R", "Capstone Expo", "95%", "computer vision"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> capstone, fall 2022. None of us had built a <strong>vision system<\/strong>. <strong>ChatGPT launched that semester<\/strong> and hooked us on AI, but there was <strong>no AI to lean on<\/strong> for code yet.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> go from zero to a detector that <strong>drives a robot<\/strong> on a moving belt by <strong>April<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> learned from <strong>docs, tutorials and trial and error<\/strong>: <strong>YOLO, OpenCV<\/strong>, labelling, training on <strong>Colab<\/strong>, and the <strong>control side<\/strong> from scratch: <strong>ESP32<\/strong>, <strong>stepper maths<\/strong> (127 steps per inch), the <strong>serial link<\/strong>, pick timing.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> a working robot at the <strong>Capstone Expo<\/strong> (<strong>95%<\/strong> removed). It’s why I chose <strong>computer vision<\/strong> for my MEng.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Backup story:<\/em> learning 3D Gaussian Splatting fast enough to reproduce a CVPR 2025 paper (card R05).<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H07", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Working on a cross-disciplinary team", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "computer engineering", "computer science", "electrical", "uneven software fundamentals"], ["T", "one system, the same way"], ["A", "in-person whiteboard sessions", "coding-assistant instructions and hooks", "which libraries, when to merge, how code is structured", "documents and posts results"], ["R", "recall 37% → 82%", "Fresh eyes paid off", "electrical intern", "AI libraries", "sped us up"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> Hitachi Rail AI project: me (<strong>computer engineering<\/strong>), a <strong>computer science<\/strong> intern and an <strong>electrical<\/strong> intern. Different strengths, <strong>uneven software fundamentals<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> get all three of us building <strong>one system, the same way<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> <strong>in-person whiteboard sessions<\/strong> on the architecture; <strong>coding-assistant instructions and hooks<\/strong> in the repo that steer <strong>which libraries, when to merge, how code is structured<\/strong>; everyone <strong>documents and posts results<\/strong> often.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> one consistent codebase everyone could extend, and measurable progress (<strong>recall 37% → 82%<\/strong>). <strong>Fresh eyes paid off:<\/strong> the <strong>electrical intern<\/strong>, new to AI, researched widely and found <strong>AI libraries<\/strong> that <strong>sped us up<\/strong>. [Recall: which library?]<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Backup story:<\/em> the capstone (mechanical, motor control and vision people on one robot).<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H08", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Leading others", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "3-intern team"], ["T", "measurable"], ["A", "split by component", "document preprocessing", "skills", "agents and workers", "assigned tasks", "reviewed the PRs", "lost in a huge, messy folder", "agent that mapped which documents and sections reference each other"], ["R", "unblocked", "recall 37% → 82%"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> I led a <strong>3-intern team<\/strong> building the AI document reviewer at Hitachi Rail.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> set direction and deliver something <strong>measurable<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> <strong>split by component<\/strong>: one intern on <strong>document preprocessing<\/strong>, one on the <strong>skills<\/strong> (the specialist checks), me on the <strong>agents and workers<\/strong> (orchestration). I <strong>assigned tasks<\/strong> and <strong>reviewed the PRs<\/strong>. When the electrical intern was <strong>lost in a huge, messy folder<\/strong> of cross-linked documents, I built him an <strong>agent that mapped which documents and sections reference each other<\/strong>, so he could pull out the ones that mattered.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> he was <strong>unblocked<\/strong>, and the system reached <strong>recall 37% → 82%<\/strong> on our fixed benchmark.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H09", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: A failure or a result that didn’t go your way", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R / lesson"], "hint2": [["S", "L3Harris", "MX-25", "how far to turn each mirror knob"], ["T", "on the real bench"], ["A", "first real run failed", "the maths was right and the code ran", "knobs were small, stiff and had no markings", "3D-printed new knobs", "more leverage", "graduation marks"], ["R / lesson", "5 min → 30 s", "in a perfect model", "real hardware", "whole system", "test on the real thing early"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> <strong>L3Harris<\/strong>, laser alignment on the <strong>MX-25<\/strong> camera. My Python tool told operators <strong>how far to turn each mirror knob<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> make it work <strong>on the real bench<\/strong>, not just in my calculations.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> the <strong>first real run failed<\/strong> even though <strong>the maths was right and the code ran<\/strong>: the <strong>knobs were small, stiff and had no markings<\/strong>, so nobody could turn them by a set amount. We <strong>3D-printed new knobs<\/strong> with <strong>more leverage<\/strong> and <strong>graduation marks<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R / lesson:<\/strong> alignment then worked: <strong>5 min → 30 s<\/strong>. Software that works <strong>in a perfect model<\/strong> can fail on <strong>real hardware<\/strong>; fix the <strong>whole system<\/strong>, and <strong>test on the real thing early<\/strong> (lab vs field at Hatch).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Backup (research):<\/em> H1 not supported: 11 of 12 checks passed, bike was 0.155 dB short of a 0.082 dB bar, and I reported it as a miss.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H10", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Attention to detail", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "publish, not to maintain", "hard-coded values", "doesn’t always match the paper"], ["T", "exactly what the code does"], ["A", "paper and code line by line", "14 differences", "hard-coded seed", "reversed", "0.5 not 0.1", "detach", "silently freezing the colour transform"], ["R", "flag", "3 seeds + noise rule", "2×2", "85 to 100%"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> research code is written to <strong>publish, not to maintain<\/strong>: <strong>hard-coded values<\/strong>, little structure, and it <strong>doesn’t always match the paper<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> know <strong>exactly what the code does<\/strong> before building on it.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> compared <strong>paper and code line by line<\/strong> and logged <strong>14 differences<\/strong>, e.g. a <strong>hard-coded seed<\/strong> (the “3 runs” were one seed), colour roles <strong>reversed<\/strong>, a loss weight of <strong>0.5 not 0.1<\/strong>, and “<strong>detach<\/strong>” <strong>silently freezing the colour transform<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> made each one a <strong>flag<\/strong> (default = original); real <strong>3 seeds + noise rule<\/strong>; a <strong>2×2<\/strong> showed the curve explains <strong>85 to 100%<\/strong> of the detach gain.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Say “research code isn’t built for production”, not “researchers can’t code”.<\/em><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H11", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Solving a problem systematically, adapting when plans change", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "monotone curve"], ["T", "measure"], ["A", "30-run diagnostic", "already monotone", "one number", "over 99%"], ["R", "dropped Rung 1", "exposure model"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> the plan was to build a <strong>monotone curve<\/strong> first (Rung 1).<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> first <strong>measure<\/strong> what the original curves actually learn.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> <strong>30-run diagnostic<\/strong>: curves were <strong>already monotone<\/strong>; views differ by <strong>one number<\/strong> that tracks brightness (<strong>over 99%<\/strong> of the variation).<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> <strong>dropped Rung 1<\/strong>, built the <strong>exposure model<\/strong> instead. Measuring first saved building the wrong thing.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H12", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Organisation under a deadline", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "scheduler", "Excel", "platform databases", "by month end"], ["T", "fast"], ["A", "broke it down", "Microsoft Copilot", "milestones", "researched each milestone", "must-have vs nice-to-have"], ["R", "by month end", "milestones → find the essential ones → plan → execute"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> Hitachi Rail: build a <strong>scheduler<\/strong> that updates <strong>Excel<\/strong> reports from several <strong>platform databases<\/strong>, needed <strong>by month end<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> deliver a working tool <strong>fast<\/strong> without missing anything critical.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> <strong>broke it down<\/strong>: gave the sites, data sources and constraints to <strong>Microsoft Copilot<\/strong> to draft <strong>milestones<\/strong>; <strong>researched each milestone<\/strong> to size it; ranked them <strong>must-have vs nice-to-have<\/strong>; refined the plan, then built in that order.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> delivered <strong>by month end<\/strong>. [Recall: the result, e.g. hours saved or how often it runs.] My rule: <strong>milestones → find the essential ones → plan → execute<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Backup (research):<\/em> ~160 GPU runs before a 5 Dec freeze; a pre-flight checklist and a resumable runner after a batch lost 11 hours to the PC sleeping.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H13", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Explaining technical work to non-experts", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "trial and error"], ["T", "trust and use"], ["A", "“how far to turn each knob”", "tested it with them"], ["R", "5 min → 30 s"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> L3Harris: operators, not engineers, aligned the MX-25 laser by <strong>trial and error<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> a tool they would <strong>trust and use<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> the output was <strong>“how far to turn each knob”<\/strong>, not maths; I <strong>tested it with them<\/strong> on the floor and changed it from their feedback.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> <strong>5 min → 30 s<\/strong> per adjustment.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H14", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: A disagreement", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "3 people", "mechanical", "two of us wanted software"], ["T", "every part had an owner"], ["A", "talked it out", "AI/vision", "motor control", "Compromises"], ["R", "Expo", "agree the interfaces early", "one number over serial"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> capstone kickoff, <strong>3 people<\/strong>: one wanted the <strong>mechanical<\/strong> side, and <strong>two of us wanted software<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> split the work so <strong>every part had an owner<\/strong> and nobody felt sidelined.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> we <strong>talked it out<\/strong>: listed every subsystem, split software into <strong>AI/vision<\/strong> (Shahaan) and <strong>motor control<\/strong> (me), mechanical and power to Dayyan. <strong>Compromises<\/strong>: not everyone got their first choice.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> every subsystem built and integrated for the <strong>Expo<\/strong>. Lesson: <strong>agree the interfaces early<\/strong> (ours was <strong>one number over serial<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H15", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "STAR: Safety", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R / line to land"], "hint2": [["S", "moving belt", "gantry", "hot motor drivers"], ["T", "safe to run and easy to stop"], ["A", "instant-stop (kill) switch", "easy-to-reach power cord", "temperature sensors", "max", "warning", "halt on a jam", "side barriers", "start-up and running warning lights"], ["R / line to land", "no incidents", "stop and ask", "no harm"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> capstone: a <strong>moving belt<\/strong>, a <strong>gantry<\/strong> and <strong>hot motor drivers<\/strong> running for long test sessions next to people.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> make it <strong>safe to run and easy to stop<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> <strong>instant-stop (kill) switch<\/strong> and an <strong>easy-to-reach power cord<\/strong>; <strong>temperature sensors<\/strong> on the electronics: fans to <strong>max<\/strong> when hot, a <strong>warning<\/strong> if too hot; <strong>halt on a jam<\/strong>; <strong>side barriers<\/strong> so nothing falls off; <strong>start-up and running warning lights<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R / line to land:<\/strong> long runs with <strong>no incidents<\/strong>. “I <strong>stop and ask<\/strong> before working on equipment I don’t know.” Hatch’s goal: <strong>no harm<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H16", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "“You haven’t used PLCs or 3D sensors.” How do you answer?", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Admit it", "Nearest thing", "How I’d close it"], "hint2": [["Admit it"], ["Nearest thing", "ESP32 motor control", "multi-view photos with camera poses", "3D image processing"], ["How I’d close it", "logic, I/O, handshake", "calibration and triangulation"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Admit it:<\/strong> I haven’t programmed a PLC or used a structured-light or laser scanner.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nearest thing:<\/strong> <strong>ESP32 motor control<\/strong> in C++ (steppers, a solenoid valve, a serial handshake with a PC); 3D reconstruction from <strong>multi-view photos with camera poses<\/strong>; a <strong>3D image processing<\/strong> course.<\/li>\n<li><strong>How I’d close it:<\/strong> I know where the PLC sits (<strong>logic, I/O, handshake<\/strong>); I’d learn from the controls engineers; <strong>calibration and triangulation<\/strong> carry over.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H17", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Design question: “How would you build a vision system for X?”", "n": 8, "star": true, "hint1": ["Requirements", "Sensor", "Lighting + protection", "Data", "Model", "Integrate", "Validate", "Maintain"], "hint2": [["Requirements", "seen", "decision", "line speed", "cost of a miss vs a false alarm"], ["Sensor", "resolution", "3 to 5 px", "global shutter"], ["Lighting + protection", "pop", "hood", "air knife"], ["Data", "on site early", "test set from other days", "known defects"], ["Model", "classical baseline first", "edge latency"], ["Integrate", "encoder or photo-eye trigger", "calibration", "PLC", "fail-safe"], ["Validate", "written first", "per condition", "shadow mode"], ["Maintain", "drift", "retrain"]], "answer": "<p><em>Mnemonic:<\/em> <strong>R<\/strong>eally <strong>S<\/strong>mart <strong>L<\/strong>ights <strong>D<\/strong>on’t <strong>M<\/strong>ake <strong>I<\/strong>ndustrial <strong>V<\/strong>ision <strong>M<\/strong>agic.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Requirements:<\/strong> what must be <strong>seen<\/strong>, what <strong>decision<\/strong> follows; smallest feature, <strong>line speed<\/strong>, latency, <strong>cost of a miss vs a false alarm<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sensor:<\/strong> 2D, 3D or thermal; <strong>resolution<\/strong> from the smallest feature (<strong>3 to 5 px<\/strong> across it); <strong>global shutter<\/strong>; exposure short enough for the speed.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Lighting + protection:<\/strong> light that makes the feature <strong>pop<\/strong>; <strong>hood<\/strong>, sealed enclosure, cooling, <strong>air knife<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Data:<\/strong> collect <strong>on site early<\/strong>, every condition; label; <strong>test set from other days<\/strong>; plant <strong>known defects<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Model:<\/strong> <strong>classical baseline first<\/strong>, ML when variation is high; sized for <strong>edge latency<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Integrate:<\/strong> <strong>encoder or photo-eye trigger<\/strong>; <strong>calibration<\/strong> to world and robot; result to the <strong>PLC<\/strong> with a handshake; <strong>fail-safe<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Validate:<\/strong> pass/fail rules <strong>written first<\/strong>; precision and recall <strong>per condition<\/strong>; lab → <strong>shadow mode<\/strong> → commissioning.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Maintain:<\/strong> watch for <strong>drift<\/strong>, clean, <strong>retrain<\/strong>, document.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n"}, {"id": "H18", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Questions to ask them", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["hardest imaging condition", "validate a vision system", "own in the first six months", "next steps"], "hint2": [["hardest imaging condition"], ["validate a vision system"], ["own in the first six months"], ["next steps"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>“What is the <strong>hardest imaging condition<\/strong> you deal with on site: dust, heat, glare, low light?”<\/li>\n<li>“How do you <strong>validate a vision system<\/strong> before a client relies on it?”<\/li>\n<li>“What would a trainee <strong>own in the first six months<\/strong>, and how is time split between office, lab and site?”<\/li>\n<li>“What are the <strong>next steps<\/strong>, and when should I expect to hear?” (Michelle is away Nov 20 to 30.)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Extra:<\/em> “What is AAR working on now: inspection, robotic tapping or sampling, telerobotics?”<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H19", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "What is your greatest strength?", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Resourcefulness", "Why it matters here", "Example"], "hint2": [["Resourcefulness", "wide", "the code itself", "AI tools", "check it with an experiment"], ["Why it matters here", "move fast", "what tools exist", "follow the field for fun"], ["Example", "detach", "diagnostics", "frozen colour transform"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Resourcefulness:<\/strong> when I’m stuck I go <strong>wide<\/strong>: papers, <strong>the code itself<\/strong>, docs, forums, colleagues, <strong>AI tools<\/strong>. Then I <strong>check it with an experiment<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why it matters here:<\/strong> AI and vision <strong>move fast<\/strong>. Knowing <strong>what tools exist<\/strong> and how to check them is half the job, and I <strong>follow the field for fun<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Example:<\/strong> the “<strong>detach<\/strong>” puzzle. The paper didn’t explain it, so I read the code, built <strong>diagnostics<\/strong>, and found the <strong>frozen colour transform<\/strong> (card H10).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "H20", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Tell me what you know about the AAR team", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["Where", "What", "Sensing", "How they work"], "hint2": [["Where", "Advanced Automation and Robotics", "Technologies", "Equipment Technology", "Mississauga", "globally"], ["What", "vision and AI", "mining, metals", "harsh and unstructured environments", "on-the-fly", "force control", "telerobotics", "out of hazards"], ["Sensing", "a one-armed blind man", "2D vision, 3D structured light, 3D laser triangulation"], ["How they work", "concept study → proof-of-concept", "ABB", "get physical fast", "design", "supply and commissioning", "lab trials and field support"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Where:<\/strong> <strong>Advanced Automation and Robotics<\/strong>, in the <strong>Technologies<\/strong> team (Business Practices: <strong>Equipment Technology<\/strong>), <strong>Mississauga<\/strong>, supporting offices <strong>globally<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>What:<\/strong> <strong>vision and AI<\/strong> for <strong>mining, metals<\/strong> and other complex operations; robots for “<strong>harsh and unstructured environments<\/strong>”: hardened, <strong>on-the-fly<\/strong> motion, <strong>force control<\/strong>, <strong>telerobotics<\/strong> that move people <strong>out of hazards<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sensing:<\/strong> their page calls a robot without sensors “<strong>a one-armed blind man<\/strong>”; they use <strong>2D vision, 3D structured light, 3D laser triangulation<\/strong>, chosen by the outcome needed.<\/li>\n<li><strong>How they work:<\/strong> <strong>concept study → proof-of-concept<\/strong> (small, cheap trials with <strong>ABB<\/strong> and local colleges: “<strong>get physical fast<\/strong>”) → <strong>design<\/strong> → <strong>supply and commissioning<\/strong>. A trainee does <strong>lab trials and field support<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Public detail is thin. Ask them what is running now (card H18).<\/em><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "H21", "sec": "H", "tag": "Hatch interview", "prompt": "Design example: vision on a mining conveyor", "n": 6, "star": true, "hint1": ["Ask first", "Sensor", "Environment", "Data", "Integration", "Validate and run"], "hint2": [["Ask first", "speed", "cost of a missed bolt (crusher damage) vs a false stop (lost tonnes)"], ["Sensor", "3D laser line profiler", "shape and height beat colour", "encoder-triggered", "metal detector"], ["Environment", "sealed hood", "LED strobe", "air knife", "vibration-isolated"], ["Data", "anomaly detection", "seeded tests", "day/night, wet/dry, ore type"], ["Integration", "edge PC", "PLC handshake", "distance ÷ belt speed", "fail-safe"], ["Validate and run", "shadow mode", "recall on seeded objects", "false stops per shift", "dirty-lens checks"]], "answer": "<p><em>Problem:<\/em> find <strong>oversize rocks and tramp metal<\/strong> on the belt feeding a <strong>crusher<\/strong>, then stop, divert or pick. It’s my capstone at mine scale.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ask first:<\/strong> belt <strong>speed<\/strong> (2 to 6 m/s), smallest object, <strong>cost of a missed bolt (crusher damage) vs a false stop (lost tonnes)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sensor:<\/strong> <strong>3D laser line profiler<\/strong> (ore is all grey and dusty, so <strong>shape and height beat colour<\/strong>) + a 2D camera, <strong>encoder-triggered<\/strong>; a <strong>metal detector<\/strong> as a second sensor.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Environment:<\/strong> <strong>sealed hood<\/strong> (blocks sun, holds the light), <strong>LED strobe<\/strong>, <strong>air knife<\/strong> on the window, <strong>vibration-isolated<\/strong> mounts.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Data:<\/strong> rare events, so <strong>anomaly detection<\/strong> (“not normal ore”) + <strong>seeded tests<\/strong> with known objects; every condition (<strong>day/night, wet/dry, ore type<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Integration:<\/strong> <strong>edge PC<\/strong> on site → <strong>PLC handshake<\/strong> → stop belt, robot or alarm; time budget = <strong>distance ÷ belt speed<\/strong>; <strong>fail-safe<\/strong> if the camera dies.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Validate and run:<\/strong> <strong>shadow mode<\/strong> beside operators; <strong>recall on seeded objects<\/strong>, <strong>false stops per shift<\/strong>; then <strong>dirty-lens checks<\/strong>, drift monitoring, retraining.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n"}, {"id": "E01", "sec": "E", "tag": "Experience", "prompt": "L3Harris: the laser calibration tool (STAR)", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "laser", "mirror knobs", "5 minutes"], ["T", "how far to turn each knob", "incoming path"], ["A", "modelled how each knob turn moves the beam", "hard to turn by a set amount", "3D-printed new knobs", "leverage and markings", "adapted the maths with the operators"], ["R", "5 min → 30 s"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> MX-25 camera: operators aligned a <strong>laser<\/strong> by turning <strong>mirror knobs<\/strong> by trial and error, about <strong>5 minutes<\/strong> each time.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> tell them <strong>how far to turn each knob<\/strong> so the beam returns along its <strong>incoming path<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> in Python, <strong>modelled how each knob turn moves the beam<\/strong>; when the real knobs proved <strong>hard to turn by a set amount<\/strong>, we <strong>3D-printed new knobs<\/strong> with <strong>leverage and markings<\/strong>, and I <strong>adapted the maths with the operators<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> <strong>5 min → 30 s<\/strong>. [Recall: how the beam offset was measured.]<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "E02", "sec": "E", "tag": "Experience", "prompt": "Hitachi Rail: AI document review (STAR)", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["S", "T", "A", "R"], "hint2": [["S", "cross-referenced engineering documents"], ["T", "3-person co-op team"], ["A", "4 LLM agents", "40 planted defects", "8 categories", "regression test"], ["R", "37% → 82%", "0.40 → 0.90"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>S:<\/strong> hundreds of <strong>cross-referenced engineering documents<\/strong>, checked for errors by hand.<\/li>\n<li><strong>T:<\/strong> lead a <strong>3-person co-op team<\/strong> building an AI reviewer.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A:<\/strong> <strong>4 LLM agents<\/strong> acting as subject experts; a benchmark of <strong>40 planted defects<\/strong> in <strong>8 categories<\/strong>, used as a <strong>regression test<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>R:<\/strong> recall <strong>37% → 82%<\/strong>. Also a RAG chatbot, MRR <strong>0.40 → 0.90<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Link to Hatch:<\/em> planting known defects is how <strong>inspection systems are validated<\/strong>.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C01", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Your capstone in one breath", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["What", "Why", "How + result"], "hint2": [["What", "CCPU", "Tim Hortons cups", "mixed recycling", "moving conveyor", "suction cup", "x–z gantry"], ["Why", "plastic liner", "aren’t recyclable", "contaminate", "sorts by hand"], ["How + result", "webcam → YOLOv3 (OpenCV DNN, GPU) → lane over USB serial → ESP32 → steppers + vacuum", "95%", "under 1%", "~100 trials"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>What:<\/strong> the <strong>CCPU<\/strong>: a robot that finds <strong>Tim Hortons cups<\/strong> in <strong>mixed recycling<\/strong> on a <strong>moving conveyor<\/strong> and lifts them out with a <strong>suction cup<\/strong> on an <strong>x–z gantry<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why:<\/strong> paper cups have a <strong>plastic liner<\/strong>, so they <strong>aren’t recyclable<\/strong> and <strong>contaminate<\/strong> the stream; Hamilton composts them but <strong>sorts by hand<\/strong>. Tim Hortons sells the most cups in Canada.<\/li>\n<li><strong>How + result:<\/strong> <strong>webcam → YOLOv3 (OpenCV DNN, GPU) → lane over USB serial → ESP32 → steppers + vacuum<\/strong>. <strong>95%<\/strong> removed, <strong>under 1%<\/strong> false positives over <strong>~100 trials<\/strong>; team of 3; Capstone Expo.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_rig.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C02", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "The pipeline, step by step", "n": 6, "star": true, "hint1": ["Capture", "Preprocess", "Detect", "Filter", "Locate + queue", "Act"], "hint2": [["Capture", "~1 m", "LED strips", "960×720", "short exposure + high gain"], ["Preprocess", "416×416", "0 to 1", "BGR → RGB"], ["Detect", "YOLOv3", "OpenCV DNN", "CUDA", "0.09 s"], ["Filter", "confidence &gt; 0.5", "NMS"], ["Locate + queue", "cm on the belt", "once", "detection zone", "22.5 cm/s"], ["Act", "lane (0 to 17 in)", "USB serial", "ESP32", "times", "lowers, sucks, carries to the bin, releases", "“done”"]], "answer": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Capture:<\/strong> webcam <strong>~1 m<\/strong> above the belt, <strong>LED strips<\/strong> along both sides, <strong>960×720<\/strong>, <strong>short exposure + high gain<\/strong> (no motion blur), autofocus off.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Preprocess:<\/strong> <code>blobFromImage<\/code>: resize to <strong>416×416<\/strong>, scale to <strong>0 to 1<\/strong> (÷255), <strong>BGR → RGB<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Detect:<\/strong> <strong>YOLOv3<\/strong>, one class (“TimsCup”), in <strong>OpenCV DNN<\/strong> on <strong>CUDA<\/strong>, ~<strong>0.09 s<\/strong> per frame.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Filter:<\/strong> keep <strong>confidence &gt; 0.5<\/strong>; <strong>NMS<\/strong> (IoU 0.4) removes duplicate boxes.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Locate + queue:<\/strong> box centre → <strong>cm on the belt<\/strong> (~0.7 mm per pixel); each cup reported <strong>once<\/strong>, from a 7 cm <strong>detection zone<\/strong>; queued and moved along at belt speed (<strong>22.5 cm/s<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Act:<\/strong> the cup’s <strong>lane (0 to 17 in)<\/strong> goes over <strong>USB serial<\/strong> to the gantry <strong>ESP32<\/strong>, which <strong>times<\/strong> the move, <strong>lowers, sucks, carries to the bin, releases<\/strong>, and replies <strong>“done”<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_system.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C03", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Dataset and training", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["912 images", "Labelled", "Trained", "10% held out"], "hint2": [["912 images", "real belt", "mixed with random recycling", "10% with no cups"], ["Labelled", "Label Studio", "AWS", "box per cup", "225 boxes an hour"], ["Trained", "Darknet", "Google Colab", "pretrained Darknet-53", "2,000 iterations", "3 h"], ["10% held out", "179 → 311 → 912"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>912 images<\/strong> in the final set, from our webcam on the <strong>real belt<\/strong>: cups (new, crushed, lid on or off) <strong>mixed with random recycling<\/strong>; about <strong>10% with no cups<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Labelled<\/strong> in <strong>Label Studio<\/strong> on an <strong>AWS<\/strong> server so all three of us could label at once; one <strong>box per cup<\/strong> (YOLO text: class, x, y, w, h); about <strong>225 boxes an hour<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Trained<\/strong> with <strong>Darknet<\/strong> on <strong>Google Colab<\/strong> GPUs from <strong>pretrained Darknet-53<\/strong> weights (transfer learning); <strong>2,000 iterations<\/strong>, about <strong>3 h<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>10% held out<\/strong> to check the loss. Three versions (<strong>179 → 311 → 912<\/strong> images), each fixing the last one’s false positives (C11).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C04", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Why YOLOv3 and OpenCV DNN?", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["One-stage detector", "OpenCV DNN"], "hint2": [["One-stage detector", "one pass", "moving belt", "mature and well documented"], ["OpenCV DNN", "Darknet weights directly", "CUDA backend", "serial link", "one Python script"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>One-stage detector<\/strong>: the whole image in <strong>one pass<\/strong>, fast enough for a <strong>moving belt<\/strong>; in 2022 it was <strong>mature and well documented<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>OpenCV DNN<\/strong> loads <strong>Darknet weights directly<\/strong> and has a <strong>CUDA backend<\/strong>, so detection, the live overlay and the <strong>serial link<\/strong> all live in <strong>one Python script<\/strong>. <em>Today:<\/em> a newer YOLO exported to ONNX or TensorRT.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C05", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "How did you time the pick?", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Fixed belt speed", "53 cm", "My ESP32 code"], "hint2": [["Fixed belt speed", "170 rpm", "22.5 cm/s", "timestamped photos and a tape measure", "distance = time"], ["53 cm", "~2.3 s"], ["My ESP32 code", "wait = 2.3 s − arm travel time − 0.55 s lowering"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Fixed belt speed:<\/strong> conveyor stepper at <strong>170 rpm<\/strong> ≈ <strong>22.5 cm/s<\/strong>, checked with <strong>timestamped photos and a tape measure<\/strong>. So <strong>distance = time<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>From the detection zone to the arm is about <strong>53 cm<\/strong>, so a cup arrives <strong>~2.3 s<\/strong> after it’s reported.<\/li>\n<li><strong>My ESP32 code:<\/strong> move across to the cup’s lane, then <strong>wait = 2.3 s − arm travel time − 0.55 s lowering<\/strong>, then lower and grab.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> get there early, wait, drop exactly when the cup arrives.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_timing.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C06", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "The GPU speed-up", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["~0.2 s → ~0.09 s", "building OpenCV from source with CUDA and cuDNN"], "hint2": [["~0.2 s → ~0.09 s", "2.3×", "CUDA backend"], ["building OpenCV from source with CUDA and cuDNN", "~0.3 s", "960×720"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>~0.2 s → ~0.09 s<\/strong> per detection (about <strong>2.3×<\/strong>) by switching OpenCV DNN to its <strong>CUDA backend<\/strong> (two lines of code).<\/li>\n<li>The hard part was <strong>building OpenCV from source with CUDA and cuDNN<\/strong> (over a week of setup). It mattered because a detection was needed every <strong>~0.3 s<\/strong> (each 7 cm of belt); the gain was modest because <strong>960×720<\/strong> is a small image.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C07", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Results, and how you measured them", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["~100 trials", "95%", "Honest gap"], "hint2": [["~100 trials", "real recycling", "manual checklist"], ["95%", "under 1%", "picks", "no observed"], ["Honest gap", "precision, recall or mAP", "fix the metrics and test set first"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>~100 trials:<\/strong> cups mixed with <strong>real recycling<\/strong> (cardboard, paper, bottles, cans), belt running, <strong>manual checklist<\/strong>: detected? picked? any false positive on the laptop?<\/li>\n<li><strong>95%<\/strong> detected and removed; <strong>under 1%<\/strong> false positives. Misses were mostly <strong>picks<\/strong> (no seal on a crumpled cup or the rim); the final model had <strong>no observed<\/strong> detection errors.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Honest gap:<\/strong> we never tracked <strong>precision, recall or mAP<\/strong> during training, and our report says so. Today I’d <strong>fix the metrics and test set first<\/strong>, as in my research.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C08", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "What was your role?", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["Lead and bridge", "Vision", "Mine alone", "Autonomy"], "hint2": [["Lead and bridge", "led", "bridge", "hardware (Dayyan)", "software (Shahaan)", "compatible"], ["Vision", "hands-on detection work"], ["Mine alone", "gantry design in Inventor", "motor control code", "ESP32", "integration"], ["Autonomy", "no-skip mode", "freezes the belt", "no cup is missed"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Lead and bridge:<\/strong> I <strong>led<\/strong> the team and was the <strong>bridge<\/strong> between <strong>hardware (Dayyan)<\/strong> and <strong>software (Shahaan)<\/strong>: I knew what each side needed and kept the system <strong>compatible<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Vision:<\/strong> much of the <strong>hands-on detection work<\/strong> with Shahaan (he owned it on paper): data, labelling, training, the detection script.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mine alone:<\/strong> the <strong>gantry design in Inventor<\/strong> (the 3D-printed parts), the <strong>motor control code<\/strong> on the <strong>ESP32<\/strong>, and the <strong>integration<\/strong> between the systems.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Autonomy:<\/strong> the <strong>no-skip mode<\/strong>: if too many cups come, the program <strong>freezes the belt<\/strong> so <strong>no cup is missed<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>In the interview: say “we” for the detector and “I” for the gantry, control, integration and no-skip mode, and be ready to go deep on any of them.<\/em><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C09", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "What went wrong? Failure modes", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["False positives", "Missed picks", "Comms", "Hardware"], "hint2": [["False positives", "anything red or orange", "negative examples"], ["Missed picks", "smooth surface", "crumpled cup", "rim", "no seal"], ["Comms", "ESP-NOW", "fixed intervals", "dropped", "wired USB serial"], ["Hardware", "skipped steps", "slower", "H-bridges overheated", "fans"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>False positives:<\/strong> early models called <strong>anything red or orange<\/strong> a cup (cans, cartons, chip bags). Fixed with <strong>negative examples<\/strong> (C11).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Missed picks:<\/strong> suction needs a <strong>smooth surface<\/strong>; a <strong>crumpled cup<\/strong> or landing on the <strong>rim<\/strong> → <strong>no seal<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Comms:<\/strong> wireless <strong>ESP-NOW<\/strong> sent at <strong>fixed intervals<\/strong>, so positions that missed a send were <strong>dropped<\/strong> → switched to <strong>wired USB serial<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hardware:<\/strong> steppers <strong>skipped steps<\/strong> when pushed too fast (lifting had to be <strong>slower<\/strong> than lowering); <strong>H-bridges overheated<\/strong> on long runs → <strong>fans<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C10", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "What would you do differently now?", "n": 5, "star": true, "hint1": ["Metrics from day one", "Encoder", "Controlled lighting", "Edge deployment", "Industrial controls"], "hint2": [["Metrics from day one", "held-out set from another day"], ["Encoder", "hardware trigger"], ["Controlled lighting"], ["Edge deployment", "ONNX / TensorRT", "Jetson"], ["Industrial controls", "PLC", "e-stop"]], "answer": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Metrics from day one:<\/strong> precision, recall and mAP on a <strong>held-out set from another day<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Encoder<\/strong> on the belt instead of an assumed speed; a <strong>hardware trigger<\/strong> for the camera.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Controlled lighting<\/strong> in a hood and fixed exposure (my research shows what varying exposure does).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Edge deployment:<\/strong> a newer YOLO → <strong>ONNX / TensorRT<\/strong> on a <strong>Jetson<\/strong>; log every pick.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Industrial controls:<\/strong> a <strong>PLC<\/strong> with an <strong>e-stop<\/strong> and guarding instead of microcontrollers.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n"}, {"id": "C11", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Fixing the false positives", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Problem", "Fix", "Result"], "hint2": [["Problem", "179 images", "thinks anything you put down is a coffee cup", "0.77 to 0.99"], ["Fix", "negatives", "no-cup images", "random recycling", "248 + 281", "912 images"], ["Result", "only the real cup (1.0)", "what you show it", "look-alikes"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Problem:<\/strong> the first model (<strong>179 images<\/strong>, cups only) “<strong>thinks anything you put down is a coffee cup<\/strong>”: cans, cartons and chip bags at <strong>0.77 to 0.99<\/strong> confidence.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Fix:<\/strong> add <strong>negatives<\/strong>: <strong>no-cup images<\/strong> (122) and <strong>random recycling<\/strong> with cups mixed in (<strong>248 + 281<\/strong>), for <strong>912 images<\/strong> in total.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result:<\/strong> the final model finds <strong>only the real cup (1.0)<\/strong>. <em>Lesson:<\/em> a model learns <strong>what you show it<\/strong>, so show it the <strong>look-alikes<\/strong> (hard negatives).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_before_after.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C12", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "How did YOLO learn to recognise our cups?", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["Supervised", "Training", "What it learns", "Head start"], "hint2": [["Supervised", "cup here", "loss", "box position, objectness and class"], ["Training", "gradient descent", "2,000 iterations"], ["What it learns", "edges and colours", "parts and patterns", "negatives", "red ≠ cup"], ["Head start", "pretrained Darknet-53", "ImageNet", "transfer learning"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Supervised:<\/strong> each box we drew says “<strong>cup here<\/strong>”. The network predicts boxes, and a <strong>loss<\/strong> scores <strong>box position, objectness and class<\/strong> against our labels.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Training:<\/strong> <strong>gradient descent<\/strong> nudges the weights to lower the loss; <strong>2,000 iterations<\/strong>, about 3 h on a Colab GPU.<\/li>\n<li><strong>What it learns:<\/strong> early layers <strong>edges and colours<\/strong>; deeper layers <strong>parts and patterns<\/strong> (red sleeve, white logo, rim, tapered shape). The <strong>negatives<\/strong> taught it <strong>red ≠ cup<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Head start:<\/strong> a <strong>pretrained Darknet-53<\/strong> backbone already knew general shapes from <strong>ImageNet<\/strong>, so ~900 images were enough (<strong>transfer learning<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/cnn.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "C13", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Camera: exposure vs motion blur", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["blurred", "LED strips along both sides", "Link to my research"], "hint2": [["blurred", "shorter exposure", "sharp but dark"], ["LED strips along both sides", "higher sensor gain", "fixed in code"], ["Link to my research", "dark, noisy images", "low-light problem", "same settings"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>At belt speed the default exposure <strong>blurred<\/strong> the cups; a <strong>shorter exposure<\/strong> (−8 or −9) made them <strong>sharp but dark<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Fix: <strong>LED strips along both sides<\/strong> of the belt, plus <strong>higher sensor gain<\/strong>; exposure, gain and autofocus <strong>fixed in code<\/strong>, so every frame looks the same.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Link to my research:<\/strong> short exposure + gain = <strong>dark, noisy images<\/strong>, the <strong>low-light problem<\/strong> I work on now. And the model must see the <strong>same settings<\/strong> in training and in use.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C14", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "How the PC talks to the ESP32s", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["USB serial", "wireless ESP-NOW"], "hint2": [["USB serial", "one number", "lane in inches", "“done”", "run / stop"], ["wireless ESP-NOW", "fixed intervals", "dropped", "Wired serial"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>USB serial<\/strong> (115200 baud): PC → gantry ESP32 sends <strong>one number<\/strong>, the cup’s <strong>lane in inches<\/strong>; gantry → PC sends <strong>“done”<\/strong>; PC → conveyor ESP32 sends <strong>run / stop<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>We first tried <strong>wireless ESP-NOW<\/strong>. It sent at <strong>fixed intervals<\/strong>, so positions that missed a send were <strong>dropped<\/strong> and cups went by. <strong>Wired serial<\/strong> fixed it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C15", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "The queue and no-skip mode", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["queue", "No-skip mode"], "hint2": [["queue", "belt speed × time", "dead reckoning", "7 cm", "duplicates within ±3 cm"], ["No-skip mode", "13 cm", "freezes the belt", "no cup is missed", "Continuous mode", "~8 s"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Python keeps a <strong>queue<\/strong> of cups, each moved along by <strong>belt speed × time<\/strong> (<strong>dead reckoning<\/strong>). A new detection runs only every <strong>7 cm<\/strong> of travel, and <strong>duplicates within ±3 cm<\/strong> are dropped.<\/li>\n<li><strong>No-skip mode:<\/strong> if a cup gets within <strong>13 cm<\/strong> of the arm while the arm is busy, the program <strong>freezes the belt<\/strong> until the arm says “done”, so <strong>no cup is missed<\/strong>. <strong>Continuous mode:<\/strong> the belt never stops. One pick cycle takes <strong>~8 s<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "C16", "sec": "C", "tag": "Capstone", "prompt": "Gantry and suction hardware", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["x–z gantry", "Vacuum", "Frame and power"], "hint2": [["x–z gantry", "2040 aluminium extrusions", "belt drives", "3 NEMA 17 steppers", "L298N H-bridges", "3D-printed"], ["Vacuum", "oilless pump", "3-way solenoid valve", "closed = suction", "open = vent", "drops"], ["Frame and power", "PVC rollers and steel axles", "wood frame", "ATX PC power supply", "$590"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>x–z gantry<\/strong> on <strong>2040 aluminium extrusions<\/strong> with <strong>belt drives<\/strong>; <strong>3 NEMA 17 steppers<\/strong> (across, up/down, conveyor) through <strong>L298N H-bridges<\/strong>; <strong>3D-printed<\/strong> brackets.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Vacuum:<\/strong> <strong>oilless pump<\/strong> (−65 kPa, 12 V, 6 W) + a suction cup; a <strong>3-way solenoid valve<\/strong> switched by the ESP32: <strong>closed = suction<\/strong>, <strong>open = vent<\/strong>, so the cup <strong>drops<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Frame and power:<\/strong> belt on <strong>PVC rollers and steel axles<\/strong> in a <strong>wood frame<\/strong>; an <strong>ATX PC power supply<\/strong> (12 V); budget about <strong>$590<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R01", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "What is the purpose of your research project?", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Take apart", "Replace", "Test"], "hint2": [["Take apart", "tone-curve machinery", "255k parameters"], ["Replace", "simple, explicit, physical model", "one shared curve + one exposure per photo", "55 to 76"], ["Test", "match", "H1", "beat", "H2"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Take apart<\/strong> Luminance-GS’s <strong>tone-curve machinery<\/strong> (about <strong>255k parameters<\/strong>) to find out what it really does.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Replace<\/strong> it with a <strong>simple, explicit, physical model<\/strong>: <strong>one shared curve + one exposure per photo<\/strong> (<strong>55 to 76<\/strong> parameters).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Test<\/strong> it: <strong>match<\/strong> the original on normal data (<strong>H1<\/strong>), <strong>beat<\/strong> it when exposure varies (<strong>H2<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> a big black box was doing something simple. I proved it, then swapped in the simple thing.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "R02", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Your model", "n": 2, "star": true, "hint1": ["L_k(x) = F(min(1, 2^{s_k} · x))", "F"], "hint2": [["L_k(x) = F(min(1, 2^{s_k} · x))", "exposure", "clip", "shared curve F"], ["F", "monotone", "32 knots", "s_k", "stops", "γ", "K + N + 1"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>L_k(x) = F(min(1, 2^{s_k} · x))<\/strong>: scale photo k by its <strong>exposure<\/strong> 2^{s_k}, <strong>clip<\/strong> at white, then apply the <strong>shared curve F<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>F:<\/strong> <strong>monotone<\/strong>, <strong>32 knots<\/strong> dense near black, F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1. <strong>s_k:<\/strong> one number per photo, in <strong>stops<\/strong>, mean 0. <strong>γ:<\/strong> one global shape prior. Total <strong>K + N + 1<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> the same camera response for every photo; each photo just had a different exposure.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/rung2_model.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "R03", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Why do you call it “physically motivated”?", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["response curve", "radiometric calibration"], "hint2": [["response curve", "exposure", "F + s_k"], ["radiometric calibration", "readable"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A real camera = a <strong>response curve<\/strong> (the same for every shot) + an <strong>exposure<\/strong> (changes per shot). That is exactly <strong>F + s_k<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>It is like <strong>radiometric calibration<\/strong>; each s_k is <strong>readable<\/strong> in stops and could be checked against the camera’s metadata.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R04", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "The rung ladder: what you did, in order", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["Rung 0", "Rung 0.5", "M1", "Rung 2"], "hint2": [["Rung 0", "measure"], ["Rung 0.5", "2×2 experiment"], ["M1", "varying-exposure set"], ["Rung 2", "shared curve + exposure", "Next"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Rung 0:<\/strong> <strong>measure<\/strong> what the original curves learn (30 runs).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Rung 0.5:<\/strong> a <strong>2×2 experiment<\/strong> to untangle why “detach” helps (30 runs).<\/li>\n<li><strong>M1:<\/strong> build the <strong>varying-exposure set<\/strong> and run the baselines on it (30 runs).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Rung 2:<\/strong> build and test <strong>shared curve + exposure<\/strong> (65 runs). <strong>Next:<\/strong> ablations (H3) by 5 Dec.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/rung_ladder.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "R05", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Reproduction: what did you find?", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["RTX 3080 Ti", "Same seed → up to 1 dB apart"], "hint2": [["RTX 3080 Ti", "9 runs"], ["Same seed → up to 1 dB apart", "hard-coded to 42", "3 seeds", "2× noise rule"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Reproduced the paper on my <strong>RTX 3080 Ti<\/strong>: <strong>9 runs<\/strong> (3 scenes × 3).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Same seed → up to 1 dB apart<\/strong> (GPU nondeterminism), and the seed was <strong>hard-coded to 42<\/strong>, so: <strong>3 seeds<\/strong> per setting and the <strong>2× noise rule<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R06", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Rung 0: what, result, meaning", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["What", "Result", "Meaning"], "hint2": [["What"], ["Result", "already monotone", "one number", "99.2 to 99.8%", "brightness", "0.97 to 1.00"], ["Meaning", "one exposure per view", "detach confound"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>What:<\/strong> 30 runs (5 scenes × official/detach × 3 seeds) measuring the learned curves.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result:<\/strong> curves <strong>already monotone<\/strong>; differences between views are <strong>one number<\/strong> (<strong>99.2 to 99.8%<\/strong> of the variation) that tracks <strong>brightness<\/strong> (|r| <strong>0.97 to 1.00<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Meaning:<\/strong> monotonicity alone wouldn’t help; <strong>one exposure per view<\/strong> captures the per-view network. Also found the <strong>detach confound<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R07", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "“Detach”: what it means and why you used it", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["What", "Attached", "Why"], "hint2": [["What", "stop the gradient", "target doesn’t move toward the render"], ["Attached", "target chases the render", "Detached", "render moves"], ["Why", "better", "+1.9 to +2.2 dB", "baseline to beat"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>What:<\/strong> use a value but <strong>stop the gradient<\/strong> flowing back into it: the render moves toward the target, but the <strong>target doesn’t move toward the render<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Attached:<\/strong> the image loss also nudges the curve, so the <strong>target chases the render<\/strong> (a chicken-and-egg loop). <strong>Detached:<\/strong> only the <strong>render moves<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why:<\/strong> detached was <strong>better<\/strong> on chair, sofa and bike (<strong>+1.9 to +2.2 dB<\/strong>), so it became the <strong>baseline to beat<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/lgs_pipeline.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "R08", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Rung 0.5: what, result, meaning", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["What", "Result", "Meaning"], "hint2": [["What", "2×2 experiment", "attached or detached", "learned or frozen"], ["Result", "curve", "85 to 100%", "bike hue flip"], ["Meaning", "detached curve + plain RGB", "detach", "not supported"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>What:<\/strong> a <strong>2×2 experiment<\/strong>: curve <strong>attached or detached<\/strong> × colour basis <strong>learned or frozen<\/strong> (60 runs in all).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result:<\/strong> detaching the <strong>curve<\/strong> explains <strong>85 to 100%<\/strong> of the gain and removes the <strong>bike hue flip<\/strong> (yellow wheel turned red); the colour basis explains little.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Meaning:<\/strong> Rung 2 uses a <strong>detached curve + plain RGB<\/strong>, compared against <strong>detach<\/strong>. The old H3 (colour basis) is <strong>not supported<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R09", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "The LOM dataset", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["5 real scenes", "low/", "Camera poses are provided"], "hint2": [["5 real scenes", "buu, chair, sofa, bike, shrub"], ["low/", "training", "high/", "evaluation only"], ["Camera poses are provided", "not used to decide"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>5 real scenes:<\/strong> <strong>buu, chair, sofa, bike, shrub<\/strong>, photographed in low light from many views.<\/li>\n<li><strong>low/<\/strong> = dark photos (<strong>training<\/strong>); <strong>high/<\/strong> = normal-light photos of the same views (<strong>evaluation only<\/strong>, on held-out views).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Camera poses are provided<\/strong>. Shrub is reported but <strong>not used to decide<\/strong> (too noisy).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R10", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "The varying-exposure set (M1)", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["known gain 2^{u_k}", "Why", "M1 finding"], "hint2": [["known gain 2^{u_k}", "−1 to +1 stops"], ["Why", "disagree on exposure", "known answer"], ["M1 finding", "wrong way", "2.2 to 2.7 dB"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Each training photo multiplied by a <strong>known gain 2^{u_k}<\/strong>, u_k spread over <strong>−1 to +1 stops<\/strong>, mean 0; test views unchanged.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why:<\/strong> real cameras <strong>disagree on exposure<\/strong>, and a <strong>known answer<\/strong> lets me check the model.<\/li>\n<li><strong>M1 finding:<\/strong> the original’s per-view curves move the <strong>wrong way<\/strong> (they follow brightness instead of correcting it); both baselines lose <strong>2.2 to 2.7 dB<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R11", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "H1: claim, rule, result", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Claim", "Rule, fixed beforehand", "Result: not supported."], "hint2": [["Claim", "matches detach"], ["Rule, fixed beforehand", "2× seed noise"], ["Result: not supported.", "11 of 12 pass", "−0.02 dB", "bike −0.155 dB", "−0.082 dB", "F’s starting point"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Claim:<\/strong> Rung 2 <strong>matches detach<\/strong> on LOM with ~255k fewer parameters.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Rule, fixed beforehand:<\/strong> in buu, chair, sofa and bike, no metric worse than <strong>2× seed noise<\/strong> (12 checks).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result: not supported.<\/strong> <strong>11 of 12 pass<\/strong>, pooled <strong>−0.02 dB<\/strong>; <strong>bike −0.155 dB<\/strong> against a <strong>−0.082 dB<\/strong> bar. Follow-up: <strong>F’s starting point<\/strong> matters (+0.3 dB on one seed, exploratory).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R12", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "H2: claim and result", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Claim", "Result: supported.", "Robust"], "hint2": [["Claim", "beats detach"], ["Result: supported.", "+2.11 dB", "5.3× noise"], ["Robust", "23.23 → 23.11 dB", "23.25 → 21.03", "0.11 vs 0.72 dB"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Claim:<\/strong> Rung 2 <strong>beats detach<\/strong> on the varying-exposure set.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result: supported.<\/strong> <strong>+2.11 dB<\/strong> brightness-matched PSNR pooled (<strong>5.3× noise<\/strong>); ahead in all 4 decision scenes; LPIPS better.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Robust:<\/strong> Rung 2 barely moves (<strong>23.23 → 23.11 dB<\/strong>) while detach drops (<strong>23.25 → 21.03<\/strong>); seed spread <strong>0.11 vs 0.72 dB<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/results_slope.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "R13", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "H3: what it is and its status", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Claim", "Status: next ablation, pending."], "hint2": [["Claim", "only the exposures s_k", "buu’s LPIPS", "without hue flips"], ["Status: next ablation, pending.", "can’t recolour part of an image", "5 Dec", "18 Dec"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Claim:<\/strong> let the image loss train <strong>only the exposures s_k<\/strong> (F stays detached). That should recover <strong>buu’s LPIPS<\/strong> lost by detaching, <strong>without hue flips<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Status: next ablation, pending.<\/strong> One number per view <strong>can’t recolour part of an image<\/strong>, so hue flips shouldn’t return. Experiments freeze <strong>5 Dec<\/strong>, report <strong>18 Dec<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R14", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Exposure leak (and the fig3_leak figure)", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Leak", "Reading the figure", "Show it?"], "hint2": [["Leak", "exposure change", "pseudo-target", "Slope 1", "bad", "Slope 0", "good"], ["Reading the figure", "gain I applied", "brighter or darker its pseudo-target", "Official 0.41, detach 0.51", "half the gain leaks", "Rung 2 is flat (0.01)"], ["Show it?", "Yes", "mechanism", "couldn’t tell the camera from the scene", "separates them by construction", "one-panel"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Leak<\/strong> = how much of a photo’s <strong>exposure change<\/strong> shows up in its <strong>pseudo-target<\/strong>. <strong>Slope 1<\/strong> = passes straight through (<strong>bad<\/strong>: views disagree). <strong>Slope 0<\/strong> = fully corrected (<strong>good<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Reading the figure:<\/strong> x = the <strong>gain I applied<\/strong> to each training photo (−1 to +1 stops); y = how much <strong>brighter or darker its pseudo-target<\/strong> came out than in the normal set. <strong>Official 0.41, detach 0.51<\/strong>: about <strong>half the gain leaks<\/strong>. <strong>Rung 2 is flat (0.01)<\/strong>: the exposure s_k absorbs it before the shared curve.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Show it?<\/strong> <strong>Yes<\/strong>, as the <strong>mechanism<\/strong> behind the +2.11 dB: “the networks <strong>couldn’t tell the camera from the scene<\/strong>; my model <strong>separates them by construction<\/strong>.” Bring the <strong>one-panel<\/strong> version; the 5-scene figure is the backup.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/leak.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "R15", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "How does Rung 2 get its exposures?", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["starts at log2(m̄ / m_k)", "already matches"], "hint2": [["starts at log2(m̄ / m_k)", "average of all photos"], ["already matches", "~0.02 stops", "sanity check", "leak"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Each s_k <strong>starts at log2(m̄ / m_k)<\/strong>: the photo’s mean brightness against the <strong>average of all photos<\/strong> (geometric mean).<\/li>\n<li>On my set that <strong>already matches<\/strong> the true gains; training moves it only <strong>~0.02 stops<\/strong>. So it’s a <strong>sanity check<\/strong>; the real finding is the <strong>leak<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R16", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Your experimental discipline", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["3 seeds", "Decision rules fixed before", "switch whose default reproduces the original exactly", "Raw and brightness-matched PSNR"], "hint2": [["3 seeds", "real only above 2× seed noise"], ["Decision rules fixed before"], ["switch whose default reproduces the original exactly", "regression tests", "self-tests"], ["Raw and brightness-matched PSNR", "append-only research log"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>3 seeds<\/strong> per setting; an effect is <strong>real only above 2× seed noise<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Decision rules fixed before<\/strong> running (H1 and H2 written down first).<\/li>\n<li>Every change behind a <strong>switch whose default reproduces the original exactly<\/strong>; <strong>regression tests<\/strong> and <strong>self-tests<\/strong> that must give exactly 0.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Raw and brightness-matched PSNR<\/strong> side by side; an <strong>append-only research log<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R17", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Limitations", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["5 scenes, 3 seeds", "synthetic", "Hue", "2D histograms only"], "hint2": [["5 scenes, 3 seeds"], ["synthetic"], ["Hue", "depends on F’s starting point"], ["2D histograms only", "2D enhancement, then 3DGS"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Only <strong>5 scenes, 3 seeds<\/strong>, so noise estimates are rough.<\/li>\n<li>Exposure changes were <strong>synthetic<\/strong> (applied to 8-bit photos), not captured by a real camera.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hue<\/strong> is worse on bike; the result <strong>depends on F’s starting point<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>In the default, F is fitted from <strong>2D histograms only<\/strong>: no rendered information reaches it, close to “<strong>2D enhancement, then 3DGS<\/strong>” with a shared curve.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R18", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "Why does this matter for Hatch?", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Exposure varies in the field", "Small physical models", "test discipline"], "hint2": [["Exposure varies in the field", "scene from camera"], ["Small physical models", "readable, fast, edge-friendly"], ["test discipline", "known-answer tests", "acceptance rules fixed beforehand"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Exposure varies in the field<\/strong> (auto-exposure, many cameras, changing light), so a model that separates <strong>scene from camera<\/strong> holds up.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Small physical models<\/strong> beat black boxes when the physics is known: <strong>readable, fast, edge-friendly<\/strong> (40% faster training here).<\/li>\n<li>The same <strong>test discipline<\/strong> as lab trials: <strong>known-answer tests<\/strong>, <strong>acceptance rules fixed beforehand<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "R19", "sec": "R", "tag": "Research project", "prompt": "The tone curve and my project, in one picture", "n": 2, "star": true, "hint1": ["Original", "Mine"], "hint2": [["Original", "own 255-number curve", "two neural networks", "black gets lifted"], ["Mine", "undo the photo’s exposure", "one shared curve F", "55 to 76 parameters"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Original:<\/strong> each photo gets its <strong>own 255-number curve<\/strong> from <strong>two neural networks<\/strong> (~255k parameters). The curves are free to differ, and <strong>black gets lifted<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mine:<\/strong> first <strong>undo the photo’s exposure<\/strong> (×2^{s_k}, one number), then apply <strong>one shared curve F<\/strong> (32 knots, F(0) = 0) to every photo. <strong>55 to 76 parameters<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> fix the camera difference first, then brighten every photo the same way.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/tone_design.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "L01", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "What does Luminance-GS do?", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Input", "Never sees a normal-light photo", "two renders"], "hint2": [["Input", "Output", "normally lit", "CVPR 2025"], ["Never sees a normal-light photo", "unsupervised", "pseudo-targets", "tone curve"], ["two renders", "dark photo", "pseudo-target", "histogram equalisation + a gamma prior", "two small networks"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Input:<\/strong> dark photos + camera poses. <strong>Output:<\/strong> a 3D scene that renders <strong>normally lit<\/strong> from any viewpoint. (Cui, Chu, Harada, <strong>CVPR 2025<\/strong>.)<\/li>\n<li><strong>Never sees a normal-light photo<\/strong>, so it is <strong>unsupervised<\/strong>: it invents <strong>pseudo-targets<\/strong> by brightening each photo with a learned <strong>tone curve<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The Gaussians learn <strong>two renders<\/strong>: one matches the <strong>dark photo<\/strong>, one matches the <strong>pseudo-target<\/strong>. The curve is shaped by <strong>histogram equalisation + a gamma prior<\/strong>, varied per view by <strong>two small networks<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L02", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Why not brighten each photo in 2D, then run 3DGS?", "n": 2, "star": true, "hint1": ["slightly different brightness and colour", "floaters and flicker"], "hint2": [["slightly different brightness and colour"], ["floaters and flicker", "together with", "consistent"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Enhancing each photo separately gives <strong>slightly different brightness and colour<\/strong> per photo.<\/li>\n<li>3DGS then explains those differences with geometry: <strong>floaters and flicker<\/strong>. Luminance-GS trains the brightening <strong>together with<\/strong> the 3D scene to keep it <strong>consistent<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L03", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Novel view synthesis", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["known camera positions", "held-out test views"], "hint2": [["known camera positions", "new viewpoint"], ["held-out test views"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Given photos from <strong>known camera positions<\/strong>, render the scene from a <strong>new viewpoint<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Scored on <strong>held-out test views<\/strong> against real photos.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L04", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "3D Gaussian Splatting", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["hundreds of thousands of 3D Gaussians", "position", "Splatted"], "hint2": [["hundreds of thousands of 3D Gaussians", "230k"], ["position", "scale", "rotation", "opacity", "colour as spherical harmonics"], ["Splatted", "trains in minutes and renders in real time"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A scene = <strong>hundreds of thousands of 3D Gaussians<\/strong> (fuzzy ellipsoids); bike ends with about <strong>230k<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Each stores <strong>position<\/strong> (3), <strong>scale<\/strong> (3) + <strong>rotation<\/strong>, <strong>opacity<\/strong>, and <strong>colour as spherical harmonics<\/strong> (colour can change with viewing angle).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Splatted<\/strong> (projected) onto the image, so it <strong>trains in minutes and renders in real time<\/strong> (NeRF instead runs a network along every ray).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/gaussians.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "L05", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "How is 3DGS trained?", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Render", "Densify", "10k steps"], "hint2": [["Render", "compare", "0.8·L1 + 0.2·(1 − SSIM)", "backpropagate"], ["Densify", "prune"], ["10k steps", "8 to 13 minutes"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Render<\/strong> a training view, <strong>compare<\/strong> with the photo (<strong>0.8·L1 + 0.2·(1 − SSIM)<\/strong>), <strong>backpropagate<\/strong>, update every Gaussian.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Densify<\/strong> (clone or split where gradients are large) and <strong>prune<\/strong> (nearly transparent ones) every 100 steps.<\/li>\n<li>Luminance-GS: <strong>10k steps<\/strong>, about <strong>8 to 13 minutes<\/strong> per scene on one GPU.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L06", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "COLMAP / Structure-from-Motion", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Matches features", "initialise the Gaussians"], "hint2": [["Matches features", "camera pose", "sparse 3D point cloud"], ["initialise the Gaussians"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Matches features<\/strong> across photos to get each photo’s <strong>camera pose<\/strong> and a <strong>sparse 3D point cloud<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The sparse points <strong>initialise the Gaussians<\/strong>. For LOM the poses come with the dataset.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L07", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Tone curve", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["function y = L(x)", "brightness and contrast", "pseudo-target"], "hint2": [["function y = L(x)", "[0, 1]", "every pixel"], ["brightness and contrast", "never moves anything"], ["pseudo-target", "baked into the Gaussians"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A <strong>function y = L(x)<\/strong> from input to output brightness, both in <strong>[0, 1]<\/strong>, applied to <strong>every pixel<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Changes <strong>brightness and contrast<\/strong> but <strong>never moves anything<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Its only job is the <strong>pseudo-target<\/strong>; its brightness and colour get <strong>baked into the Gaussians<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> a dial that says “this dark value becomes that bright value”.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/tone_curve.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "L08", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Look-up table (LUT)", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["list of numbers", "“find this pixel’s entry, read it out”"], "hint2": [["list of numbers", "255 entries"], ["“find this pixel’s entry, read it out”"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Stores the curve as a <strong>list of numbers<\/strong>: entry i = output for input level i. Luminance-GS uses <strong>255 entries<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Applying it = <strong>“find this pixel’s entry, read it out”<\/strong>. Fast, no maths per pixel.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L09", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Monotone curve", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Non-decreasing", "tones flip"], "hint2": [["Non-decreasing", "never gives a darker output"], ["tones flip", "already monotone", "enforces"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Non-decreasing<\/strong>: a brighter input <strong>never gives a darker output<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>If not monotone, <strong>tones flip<\/strong> (a darker shade ends up brighter). The original curves were <strong>already monotone<\/strong> (Rung 0); Rung 2 <strong>enforces<\/strong> it.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/gamma_s_monotone.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "L10", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Histogram and CDF", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Histogram", "CDF(v)", "bunched near black"], "hint2": [["Histogram", "255 bins"], ["CDF(v)", "fraction of pixels ≤ v", "0 to 1", "never decreases"], ["bunched near black", "shoots up early"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Histogram:<\/strong> how many pixels have each brightness (<strong>255 bins<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>CDF(v):<\/strong> the <strong>fraction of pixels ≤ v<\/strong>; rises from <strong>0 to 1<\/strong>, <strong>never decreases<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>In a dark photo the histogram is <strong>bunched near black<\/strong>, so the CDF <strong>shoots up early<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L11", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Histogram equalisation (HE)", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["CDF as the tone curve", "whole range", "Too aggressive alone"], "hint2": [["CDF as the tone curve", "90%", "0.15 → 0.9"], ["whole range", "much brighter"], ["Too aggressive alone", "noise", "posterises", "blends it with a smooth gamma prior"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Uses the <strong>CDF as the tone curve<\/strong>: v → CDF(v). If <strong>90%<\/strong> of pixels are below 0.15, then <strong>0.15 → 0.9<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Spreads values over the <strong>whole range<\/strong>, so a dark photo gets <strong>much brighter<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Too aggressive alone:<\/strong> amplifies <strong>noise<\/strong> and <strong>posterises<\/strong> (few levels stretched over the whole range), so Luminance-GS <strong>blends it with a smooth gamma prior<\/strong>. CLAHE = a local version with a clip limit.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/hist_he.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "L12", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Gamma curve, S-curve, shape prior", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Gamma", "S-curve", "Shape prior P_k"], "hint2": [["Gamma", "γ &lt; 1 brightens"], ["S-curve", "more contrast"], ["Shape prior P_k", "only γ moves"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Gamma:<\/strong> y = x^γ. <strong>γ &lt; 1 brightens<\/strong> the darks; γ = 1 does nothing.<\/li>\n<li><strong>S-curve:<\/strong> darkens below a midpoint α, brightens above it, so <strong>more contrast<\/strong>; β sets the strength.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shape prior P_k<\/strong> = S-curve(gamma(identity)) per view; in practice <strong>only γ moves<\/strong> (to about 0.1).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L13", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Pseudo-target and “unsupervised”", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Pseudo-target", "Unsupervised", "enhanced render"], "hint2": [["Pseudo-target", "the dark photo passed through the learned curve"], ["Unsupervised", "input + priors"], ["enhanced render", "only the enhanced Gaussians"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Pseudo-target:<\/strong> a stand-in answer = <strong>the dark photo passed through the learned curve<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Unsupervised:<\/strong> no ground truth; targets come from the <strong>input + priors<\/strong> (assumptions such as “use the whole range” and “keep the curve smooth”).<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>enhanced render<\/strong> trains toward it; at test time <strong>only the enhanced Gaussians<\/strong> are used.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L14", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "How the original builds each view’s curve", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Global curve L_g", "Curve bias L_b,k", "colour basis"], "hint2": [["Global curve L_g", "255-value", "identity"], ["Curve bias L_b,k", "per-view correction", "cross-attention network"], ["colour basis", "shape network", "255k parameters"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Global curve L_g:<\/strong> one learnable <strong>255-value<\/strong> curve shared by all views, starting at the <strong>identity<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Curve bias L_b,k:<\/strong> a 255-value <strong>per-view correction<\/strong> from a <strong>cross-attention network<\/strong> (photo + pose). L_k = clamp(L_g + L_b,k).<\/li>\n<li>Plus a per-view <strong>colour basis<\/strong> and a <strong>shape network<\/strong> for the prior: about <strong>255k parameters<\/strong> in all (bias net 143,743 + shape net 111,235).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L15", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "Cross-attention in Luminance-GS", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Query", "the pose decides which parts of the photo to look at"], "hint2": [["Query", "pose", "keys and values", "image features"], ["the pose decides which parts of the photo to look at"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Query<\/strong> = the camera <strong>pose<\/strong>; <strong>keys and values<\/strong> = <strong>image features<\/strong> (16 tokens from a small CNN).<\/li>\n<li>Intuition: <strong>the pose decides which parts of the photo to look at<\/strong>; the output is the per-view curve bias.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L16", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "The dark branch", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["dark version", "one dark colour per Gaussian for all views"], "hint2": [["dark version", "dark photo", "real data"], ["one dark colour per Gaussian for all views", "different exposures", "3.6 to 11.9 dB", "2^{−s_k}", "+5.8 to +7.6 dB"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The Gaussians also render a <strong>dark version<\/strong> that must match the <strong>dark photo<\/strong>, which keeps the scene tied to <strong>real data<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>It has <strong>one dark colour per Gaussian for all views<\/strong>, so it fails when photos have <strong>different exposures<\/strong> (fit drops <strong>3.6 to 11.9 dB<\/strong>). My fix scales each view’s dark render by <strong>2^{−s_k}<\/strong>: <strong>+5.8 to +7.6 dB<\/strong> fit.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "L17", "sec": "L", "tag": "Luminance-GS original", "prompt": "The four losses", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["L_low", "L_enh (×0.5)", "L_spa", "L_hist (×10)"], "hint2": [["L_low", "dark photo"], ["L_enh (×0.5)", "pseudo-target"], ["L_spa", "local contrast"], ["L_hist (×10)", "HE-CDF", "smoothness", "shape prior", "dominates", "mainly by the priors"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>L_low:<\/strong> dark render vs <strong>dark photo<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>L_enh (×0.5):<\/strong> enhanced render vs <strong>pseudo-target<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>L_spa:<\/strong> keeps the enhanced render’s <strong>local contrast<\/strong> consistent with the dark photo.<\/li>\n<li><strong>L_hist (×10):<\/strong> curve vs <strong>HE-CDF<\/strong> + <strong>smoothness<\/strong> + <strong>shape prior<\/strong>. It <strong>dominates<\/strong> (about 5× the image terms), so the curve is shaped <strong>mainly by the priors<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "M01", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "PSNR and dB", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["PSNR = 10 · log10(1 / MSE)", "dB is a log scale", "Pixel by pixel"], "hint2": [["PSNR = 10 · log10(1 / MSE)", "Higher = closer"], ["dB is a log scale", "+3 dB ≈ half the error", "+1 dB ≈ 21% less"], ["Pixel by pixel", "punishes brightness offsets"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>PSNR = 10 · log10(1 / MSE)<\/strong> for images in [0, 1]. <strong>Higher = closer<\/strong> to the ground truth.<\/li>\n<li><strong>dB is a log scale:<\/strong> <strong>+3 dB ≈ half the error<\/strong>; <strong>+1 dB ≈ 21% less<\/strong> squared error; +10 dB = 10× less.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Pixel by pixel:<\/strong> ignores structure and <strong>punishes brightness offsets<\/strong> hard. About 20 dB is fair, 30 dB is very good.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "M02", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "Brightness-matched PSNR", "n": 2, "star": true, "hint1": ["Scale the render to the ground truth’s mean brightness", "Why"], "hint2": [["Scale the render to the ground truth’s mean brightness"], ["Why", "can’t be known", "detail and colour", "exposure", "next to raw PSNR"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Scale the render to the ground truth’s mean brightness<\/strong>, then take PSNR.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why:<\/strong> with no normal-light training images, overall brightness <strong>can’t be known<\/strong>; this separates <strong>detail and colour<\/strong> from <strong>exposure<\/strong>. Always shown <strong>next to raw PSNR<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "M03", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "SSIM", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Structural similarity", "structure"], "hint2": [["Structural similarity", "local mean, contrast and structure", "0 to 1"], ["structure"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Structural similarity:<\/strong> compares <strong>local mean, contrast and structure<\/strong> in small windows; <strong>0 to 1<\/strong>, 1 = identical.<\/li>\n<li>Closer than PSNR to how people judge <strong>structure<\/strong>; also part of the 3DGS loss.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "M04", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "LPIPS", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Learned perceptual distance", "human judgement"], "hint2": [["Learned perceptual distance", "deep-network features", "lower = better"], ["human judgement"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Learned perceptual distance:<\/strong> compares <strong>deep-network features<\/strong> (VGG or AlexNet) of two images; <strong>lower = better<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Best of the three at matching <strong>human judgement<\/strong> of blur and texture.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "M05", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "Precision, recall, F1", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Precision = TP / (TP + FP)", "Recall = TP / (TP + FN)", "F1"], "hint2": [["Precision = TP / (TP + FP)", "how much was right"], ["Recall = TP / (TP + FN)", "how much I found"], ["F1", "cost of a miss vs a false alarm"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Precision = TP / (TP + FP):<\/strong> of what I flagged, <strong>how much was right<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Recall = TP / (TP + FN):<\/strong> of what was there, <strong>how much I found<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>F1<\/strong> = their harmonic mean. Set the threshold by the <strong>cost of a miss vs a false alarm<\/strong> (in inspection, misses usually cost more).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/confusion.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "M06", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "IoU and mAP", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["IoU", "AP", "mAP"], "hint2": [["IoU", "IoU ≥ 0.5"], ["AP", "precision-recall curve"], ["mAP", "mean AP over classes"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>IoU<\/strong> = overlap area / union area of predicted and true boxes; a detection counts as correct if <strong>IoU ≥ 0.5<\/strong> (common choice).<\/li>\n<li><strong>AP<\/strong> = area under the <strong>precision-recall curve<\/strong> for one class.<\/li>\n<li><strong>mAP<\/strong> = <strong>mean AP over classes<\/strong> (COCO also averages over IoU 0.5 to 0.95).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/iou.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "M07", "sec": "M", "tag": "Metrics", "prompt": "TP, FP and FN for a detector", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["TP", "No TN", "Threshold trade-off"], "hint2": [["TP", "FP", "no cup", "duplicate", "FN", "no box"], ["No TN", "precision", "recall"], ["Threshold trade-off", "fewer FPs, more misses", "picking recycling", "false belt stop", "crusher damage"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>TP:<\/strong> a box on a real cup (IoU ≥ 0.5 with the label). <strong>FP:<\/strong> a box on <strong>no cup<\/strong> (a Coke can) or a <strong>duplicate<\/strong>. <strong>FN:<\/strong> a cup with <strong>no box<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>No TN<\/strong> in detection: “no box on empty belt” happens everywhere, so we use <strong>precision<\/strong> (TP ÷ all boxes) and <strong>recall<\/strong> (TP ÷ all cups) instead of accuracy.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Threshold trade-off:<\/strong> raise confidence → <strong>fewer FPs, more misses<\/strong>. Capstone: an FP = <strong>picking recycling<\/strong>. Mine site: an FP = <strong>false belt stop<\/strong>, an FN = <strong>crusher damage<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F01", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Digital image basics", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["pixels", "8-bit = 256 levels", "mm per pixel"], "hint2": [["pixels", "3 channels"], ["8-bit = 256 levels", "[0, 1]"], ["mm per pixel", "3+ pixels"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A grid of <strong>pixels<\/strong>; colour images have <strong>3 channels<\/strong> (R, G, B).<\/li>\n<li><strong>8-bit = 256 levels<\/strong> (0 to 255) per channel; networks usually use <strong>[0, 1]<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>mm per pixel<\/strong> sets the smallest defect you can see (about <strong>3+ pixels<\/strong> across it).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F02", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Colour spaces", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["RGB", "hue flip"], "hint2": [["RGB", "HSV", "Hue", "Saturation", "Value", "Grayscale"], ["hue flip", "yellow wheel turning red"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>RGB<\/strong> is how sensors store colour. <strong>HSV<\/strong>: <strong>Hue<\/strong> (colour angle), <strong>Saturation<\/strong>, <strong>Value<\/strong>, so colour thresholds are easier. <strong>Grayscale<\/strong> for most inspection.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>hue flip<\/strong> (my research) = a colour changing identity, e.g. a <strong>yellow wheel turning red<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F03", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Exposure and stops", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["shutter time × aperture", "1 stop = 2× the light", "fix the exposure"], "hint2": [["shutter time × aperture", "gain (ISO)"], ["1 stop = 2× the light", "−1 = half, +1 = double"], ["fix the exposure"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Exposure = light collected: <strong>shutter time × aperture<\/strong>, then <strong>gain (ISO)<\/strong> amplifies it.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1 stop = 2× the light<\/strong>; 2^{s} scales by s stops (<strong>−1 = half, +1 = double<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li>In inspection, <strong>fix the exposure<\/strong> so features look the same in every frame; auto-exposure changes the image.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F04", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Dynamic range, clipping, gamma", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Dynamic range", "linear", "HDR"], "hint2": [["Dynamic range", "clipping"], ["linear", "gamma / sRGB"], ["HDR"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Dynamic range:<\/strong> darkest to brightest the sensor can record; beyond it, <strong>clipping<\/strong> (detail lost).<\/li>\n<li>Sensors are <strong>linear<\/strong>; <strong>gamma / sRGB<\/strong> compresses values for display.<\/li>\n<li>Bright welds next to dark background → <strong>HDR<\/strong> or several exposures.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F05", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Noise in low light", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Shot noise", "Brightening amplifies noise"], "hint2": [["Shot noise", "read noise", "SNR"], ["Brightening amplifies noise", "frame averaging"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Shot noise<\/strong> (random photon arrivals, grows with √signal) + <strong>read noise<\/strong> (fixed, electronics), so low light = low <strong>SNR<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Brightening amplifies noise<\/strong>. Fixes: more light, longer exposure, <strong>frame averaging<\/strong>, denoising.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F06", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Smoothing filters", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Gaussian blur", "Median filter"], "hint2": [["Gaussian blur", "convolution", "softens edges"], ["Median filter", "salt-and-pepper", "keeps edges"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Gaussian blur:<\/strong> a weighted average (a <strong>convolution<\/strong>); removes fine noise but <strong>softens edges<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Median filter:<\/strong> the middle value in each window; kills <strong>salt-and-pepper<\/strong> noise and <strong>keeps edges<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F07", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Edge detection", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Sobel", "Canny", "measure dimensions"], "hint2": [["Sobel", "x and y gradients"], ["Canny", "thin the edges", "two thresholds"], ["measure dimensions", "cracks"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Sobel:<\/strong> convolution kernels for the <strong>x and y gradients<\/strong>, giving edge strength and direction.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Canny:<\/strong> blur → gradient → <strong>thin the edges<\/strong> (non-max suppression) → <strong>two thresholds<\/strong> (hysteresis).<\/li>\n<li>Uses: <strong>measure dimensions<\/strong>, find <strong>cracks<\/strong>, locate parts.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F08", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Thresholding and blob analysis", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Threshold", "Adaptive threshold", "connected components / contours"], "hint2": [["Threshold", "Otsu"], ["Adaptive threshold"], ["connected components / contours", "area, centroid, bounding box"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Threshold:<\/strong> pixel &gt; T → foreground. <strong>Otsu<\/strong> picks T automatically from the histogram (best split between two peaks).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Adaptive threshold<\/strong> when the lighting is uneven.<\/li>\n<li>Then <strong>connected components / contours<\/strong>: count, <strong>area, centroid, bounding box<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F09", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Morphology", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Erosion", "Opening", "clean up binary masks"], "hint2": [["Erosion", "dilation"], ["Opening", "removes noise", "Closing", "fills holes"], ["clean up binary masks"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Erosion<\/strong> shrinks white regions (removes specks); <strong>dilation<\/strong> grows them (fills gaps).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Opening<\/strong> = erode then dilate: <strong>removes noise<\/strong>. <strong>Closing<\/strong> = dilate then erode: <strong>fills holes<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Used to <strong>clean up binary masks<\/strong> before measuring.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/morphology.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F10", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Features and matching", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Keypoints + descriptors", "RANSAC", "SfM / COLMAP"], "hint2": [["Keypoints + descriptors", "SIFT", "ORB", "matched"], ["RANSAC", "most inliers"], ["SfM / COLMAP"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Keypoints + descriptors<\/strong> (<strong>SIFT<\/strong>, <strong>ORB<\/strong>) are <strong>matched<\/strong> across images.<\/li>\n<li><strong>RANSAC:<\/strong> fit the model to random minimal samples, keep the fit with the <strong>most inliers<\/strong>, so bad matches don’t matter.<\/li>\n<li>Used in <strong>SfM / COLMAP<\/strong>, image stitching, pose estimation.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F11", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Pinhole camera model", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["x = f · X / Z + cx", "Intrinsics K", "Extrinsics [R | t]"], "hint2": [["x = f · X / Z + cx"], ["Intrinsics K", "f", "(cx, cy)", "lens distortion"], ["Extrinsics [R | t]", "sits and points"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A 3D point (X, Y, Z) lands at pixel <strong>x = f · X / Z + cx<\/strong> (similar triangles).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Intrinsics K:<\/strong> focal length <strong>f<\/strong>, principal point <strong>(cx, cy)<\/strong>, plus <strong>lens distortion<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Extrinsics [R | t]:<\/strong> where the camera <strong>sits and points<\/strong> in the world.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/pinhole.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F12", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Camera calibration", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["checkerboard or ChArUco board", "intrinsics + distortion", "reprojection error"], "hint2": [["checkerboard or ChArUco board", "cv2.calibrateCamera"], ["intrinsics + distortion"], ["reprojection error", "under ~0.5 px", "Recalibrate"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Show a <strong>checkerboard or ChArUco board<\/strong> in many poses → <strong>cv2.calibrateCamera<\/strong> (Zhang’s method).<\/li>\n<li>Solves the <strong>intrinsics + distortion<\/strong> (radial, tangential) and each view’s pose.<\/li>\n<li>Check the <strong>reprojection error<\/strong> (good: <strong>under ~0.5 px<\/strong>). <strong>Recalibrate<\/strong> after any lens or mount change.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F13", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Homography", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["3×3 matrix", "pixel → belt coordinates"], "hint2": [["3×3 matrix", "plane", "4 point pairs"], ["pixel → belt coordinates"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A <strong>3×3 matrix<\/strong> that maps one <strong>plane<\/strong> to another; needs <strong>4 point pairs<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Use: <strong>pixel → belt coordinates<\/strong> on a flat conveyor, or a top-down view.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F14", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Stereo vision", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["baseline B", "Depth Z = f · B / d", "texture"], "hint2": [["baseline B", "disparity d"], ["Depth Z = f · B / d"], ["texture", "projected pattern"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Two cameras a <strong>baseline B<\/strong> apart see the same point shifted by a <strong>disparity d<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Depth Z = f · B / d<\/strong>: near = big disparity, far = small.<\/li>\n<li>Needs <strong>texture<\/strong> to match; fails on plain or shiny surfaces, so add a <strong>projected pattern<\/strong> (active stereo).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/stereo.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F15", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Structured light and laser triangulation", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Structured light", "Laser triangulation", "encoder"], "hint2": [["Structured light", "patterns", "bend", "close up", "ambient light and shiny parts"], ["Laser triangulation", "laser line", "angle", "shifts the line", "one profile per frame"], ["encoder", "Both are named on Hatch’s robotics page."]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Structured light:<\/strong> project known <strong>patterns<\/strong>; the camera sees how they <strong>bend<\/strong> → dense 3D. Accurate <strong>close up<\/strong>; hurt by <strong>ambient light and shiny parts<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Laser triangulation:<\/strong> a <strong>laser line<\/strong> on the part, a camera at an <strong>angle<\/strong>; height <strong>shifts the line<\/strong> in the image → <strong>one profile per frame<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>On a conveyor: profiles + <strong>encoder<\/strong> = a full 3D scan. <strong>Both are named on Hatch’s robotics page.<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/laser_triangulation.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F16", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Time-of-flight and LiDAR", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["ToF camera", "LiDAR"], "hint2": [["ToF camera", "light travel time", "lower resolution", "multipath"], ["LiDAR", "long-range"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>ToF camera:<\/strong> measures <strong>light travel time<\/strong> per pixel → a depth image; fast, <strong>lower resolution<\/strong>, <strong>multipath<\/strong> errors.<\/li>\n<li><strong>LiDAR:<\/strong> a scanning laser ToF → <strong>long-range<\/strong> point clouds (mine vehicles, stockpiles).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F17", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Point cloud processing", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Downsample", "RANSAC plane fit", "ICP"], "hint2": [["Downsample", "remove outliers"], ["RANSAC plane fit"], ["ICP", "clustering"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Downsample<\/strong> (voxel grid) and <strong>remove outliers<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>RANSAC plane fit<\/strong> to remove the floor or belt.<\/li>\n<li><strong>ICP<\/strong> to align two clouds; <strong>clustering<\/strong> to separate objects.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F18", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Classification vs detection vs segmentation", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Classification", "Detection", "Segmentation"], "hint2": [["Classification", "one label"], ["Detection", "box + label"], ["Segmentation", "per pixel"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Classification:<\/strong> <strong>one label<\/strong> for the image (“cup”).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Detection:<\/strong> a <strong>box + label<\/strong> per object (YOLO).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Segmentation:<\/strong> a label <strong>per pixel<\/strong> (semantic = class; instance = each object separately).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/cls_det_seg.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F19", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "YOLO and non-max suppression", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["What", "Used for", "Non-max suppression (NMS)", "confidence threshold"], "hint2": [["What", "You Only Look Once", "one-stage detector", "grid", "boxes + objectness + class", "one pass", "real time"], ["Used for", "objects on a line or a site", "PPE"], ["Non-max suppression (NMS)", "IoU above a threshold", "most confident"], ["confidence threshold", "precision against recall"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>What:<\/strong> “<strong>You Only Look Once<\/strong>”, a <strong>one-stage detector<\/strong>. A <strong>grid<\/strong> over the image; each cell predicts <strong>boxes + objectness + class<\/strong> in <strong>one pass<\/strong>, so it runs in <strong>real time<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Used for:<\/strong> fast detection of <strong>objects on a line or a site<\/strong>: parts, people and vehicles, <strong>PPE<\/strong>, defects, our cups.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Non-max suppression (NMS):<\/strong> of boxes that overlap (<strong>IoU above a threshold<\/strong>, 0.4 for us), keep the <strong>most confident<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>confidence threshold<\/strong> (0.5 for us) trades <strong>precision against recall<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/yolo_grid.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F20", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Anomaly detection for rare defects", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["rare", "autoencoder reconstruction error"], "hint2": [["rare", "good parts only"], ["autoencoder reconstruction error", "PatchCore"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Defects are <strong>rare<\/strong>, so train on <strong>good parts only<\/strong> and flag anything that looks different.<\/li>\n<li>Methods: <strong>autoencoder reconstruction error<\/strong>, <strong>PatchCore<\/strong> (feature distances). Useful when you can’t collect many defect images.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F21", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Motion blur and shutters", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Blur (px) = speed × exposure time ÷ (mm per px)", "shorter exposure + a strobe", "Global shutter"], "hint2": [["Blur (px) = speed × exposure time ÷ (mm per px)", "under 1 px", "2 px"], ["shorter exposure + a strobe"], ["Global shutter", "rolling shutter"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Blur (px) = speed × exposure time ÷ (mm per px)<\/strong>; keep it <strong>under 1 px<\/strong>. Example: 1 m/s, 1 ms, 0.5 mm/px → <strong>2 px<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Fix: <strong>shorter exposure + a strobe<\/strong> light.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Global shutter<\/strong> (all pixels at once) for moving parts; a <strong>rolling shutter<\/strong> skews them.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F22", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Machine-vision lighting", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["Backlight", "Dome / diffuse", "Low-angle (dark-field)", "Coaxial / polarisers"], "hint2": [["Backlight", "edges and dimensions"], ["Dome / diffuse", "shiny or curved"], ["Low-angle (dark-field)", "scratches and dents"], ["Coaxial / polarisers", "glare", "“lighting first, algorithm second”"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Backlight:<\/strong> a silhouette → <strong>edges and dimensions<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dome / diffuse:<\/strong> even light → <strong>shiny or curved<\/strong> parts without glare.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Low-angle (dark-field):<\/strong> grazing light makes <strong>scratches and dents<\/strong> light up.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Coaxial / polarisers:<\/strong> cut <strong>glare<\/strong> on flat shiny surfaces. Rule: <strong>“lighting first, algorithm second”<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/lighting.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F23", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Image preprocessing: what’s necessary?", "n": 4, "star": true, "hint1": ["Must match training", "Camera side", "Training only, augmentation", "Classical clean-up"], "hint2": [["Must match training", "size", "scaling", "channel order", "silent bug"], ["Camera side", "fix exposure, gain, focus and white balance", "crop to the region of interest", "undistort"], ["Training only, augmentation", "hue / saturation / exposure jitter"], ["Classical clean-up", "denoise", "contrast", "colour space"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Must match training:<\/strong> same <strong>size<\/strong> (416×416), <strong>scaling<\/strong> (0 to 1) and <strong>channel order<\/strong> (RGB). A mismatch is a <strong>silent bug<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Camera side:<\/strong> <strong>fix exposure, gain, focus and white balance<\/strong> so images don’t drift; <strong>crop to the region of interest<\/strong>; <strong>undistort<\/strong> if you measure.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Training only, augmentation:<\/strong> flips, crops, scale, <strong>hue / saturation / exposure jitter<\/strong> (Darknet’s YOLOv3 config does these by default), so the model sees more variety.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Classical clean-up<\/strong> when needed: <strong>denoise<\/strong> (Gaussian, median), <strong>contrast<\/strong> (histogram equalisation, CLAHE), <strong>colour space<\/strong> (HSV for colour thresholds).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/preprocess.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "F24", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "OpenCV", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["computer vision library", "camera capture", "In my capstone"], "hint2": [["computer vision library", "Python"], ["camera capture", "filters, edges, thresholds, contours", "calibration", "DNN module"], ["In my capstone", "CUDA"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The standard open-source <strong>computer vision library<\/strong> (C++ with <strong>Python<\/strong> bindings).<\/li>\n<li>Does: <strong>camera capture<\/strong>, resize, colour conversion, <strong>filters, edges, thresholds, contours<\/strong>, <strong>calibration<\/strong>, drawing, and a <strong>DNN module<\/strong> that runs trained networks.<\/li>\n<li><strong>In my capstone:<\/strong> <code>VideoCapture<\/code> (exposure, gain), <code>blobFromImage<\/code>, <code>dnn.readNet<\/code> + <strong>CUDA<\/strong>, <code>NMSBoxes<\/code>, then <code>rectangle<\/code> / <code>putText<\/code> / <code>imshow<\/code> for the live overlay.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "F25", "sec": "F", "tag": "CV fundamentals", "prompt": "Using YOLO, start to finish", "n": 5, "star": false, "hint1": ["Collect", "Label", "Train", "Choose thresholds", "Deploy and measure"], "hint2": [["Collect", "in the real setting", "no objects"], ["Label"], ["Train", "pretrained weights", "test set", "loss and mAP"], ["Choose thresholds", "cost of misses vs false alarms"], ["Deploy and measure", "ONNX / TensorRT", "latency"]], "answer": "<ol>\n<li><strong>Collect<\/strong> images <strong>in the real setting<\/strong>, including some with <strong>no objects<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Label<\/strong> boxes (Label Studio, CVAT, Roboflow) → YOLO text format: class, x, y, w, h (0 to 1).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Train<\/strong> from <strong>pretrained weights<\/strong>; hold out a <strong>test set<\/strong>; watch the <strong>loss and mAP<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Choose thresholds<\/strong> (confidence, NMS) on held-out data, by the <strong>cost of misses vs false alarms<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Deploy and measure:<\/strong> export (we used Darknet → OpenCV DNN; today <strong>ONNX / TensorRT<\/strong>), check <strong>latency<\/strong> on the real hardware, wire the output to the machine.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><em>Today, in a few lines with Ultralytics:<\/em> <code>model = YOLO(&quot;yolo11n.pt&quot;)<\/code>, <code>model.train(data=&quot;cups.yaml&quot;)<\/code>, <code>model.predict(frame)<\/code>.<\/p>\n"}, {"id": "I01", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "PLC", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Programmable Logic Controller", "ladder logic or structured text", "results"], "hint2": [["Programmable Logic Controller", "deterministic logic", "scan cycle"], ["ladder logic or structured text", "Allen-Bradley (Rockwell)", "Siemens"], ["results", "decides and actuates", "safety interlocks"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Programmable Logic Controller:<\/strong> a rugged industrial computer running the machine’s <strong>deterministic logic<\/strong> in a fast <strong>scan cycle<\/strong> (read inputs → run logic → write outputs).<\/li>\n<li>Programmed in <strong>ladder logic or structured text<\/strong> (IEC 61131-3); main vendors <strong>Allen-Bradley (Rockwell)<\/strong> and <strong>Siemens<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The vision PC sends it <strong>results<\/strong>; the PLC <strong>decides and actuates<\/strong>, and owns the <strong>safety interlocks<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I02", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Industrial communication", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Digital I/O", "EtherNet/IP"], "hint2": [["Digital I/O", "trigger and pass/fail"], ["EtherNet/IP", "PROFINET", "Modbus TCP", "OPC UA"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Digital I/O<\/strong> (24 V) for a simple <strong>trigger and pass/fail<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Industrial Ethernet: <strong>EtherNet/IP<\/strong> (Rockwell), <strong>PROFINET<\/strong> (Siemens), <strong>Modbus TCP<\/strong> (simple), <strong>OPC UA<\/strong> (data to plant IT systems).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I03", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Vision-to-PLC handshake", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Trigger", "Result", "heartbeat / watchdog"], "hint2": [["Trigger", "Busy"], ["Result", "Result Valid", "acknowledges"], ["heartbeat / watchdog", "fail-safe default"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The PLC sets <strong>Trigger<\/strong> → vision sets <strong>Busy<\/strong>, grabs and processes the image.<\/li>\n<li>Vision writes the <strong>Result<\/strong> and sets <strong>Result Valid<\/strong> → the PLC reads it and <strong>acknowledges<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>heartbeat / watchdog<\/strong> and a <strong>fail-safe default<\/strong> (no result in time → reject or stop).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/plc_handshake.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "I04", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Encoders and hardware triggers", "n": 2, "star": true, "hint1": ["Encoder", "photo-eye or encoder count triggers the camera"], "hint2": [["Encoder", "exact position"], ["photo-eye or encoder count triggers the camera", "tracked from camera to actuator"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Encoder:<\/strong> pulses per millimetre of belt travel, so you know the <strong>exact position<\/strong> even if the speed changes.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>photo-eye or encoder count triggers the camera<\/strong> at the right spot, and the part is <strong>tracked from camera to actuator<\/strong> by counts.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I05", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Edge deployment", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Export", "NVIDIA Jetson", "worst-case latency"], "hint2": [["Export", "ONNX", "TensorRT", "FP16 / INT8"], ["NVIDIA Jetson", "fanless, sealed"], ["worst-case latency", "throughput", "watchdog and logging"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Export<\/strong> the model (PyTorch → <strong>ONNX<\/strong>), optimise it (<strong>TensorRT<\/strong>, OpenVINO), use <strong>FP16 / INT8<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Hardware: <strong>NVIDIA Jetson<\/strong> or an industrial PC with a GPU; <strong>fanless, sealed<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Measure <strong>worst-case latency<\/strong> (not the average) and <strong>throughput<\/strong>; add a <strong>watchdog and logging<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I06", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Hand-eye calibration", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["camera frame and the robot frame", "AX = XB"], "hint2": [["camera frame and the robot frame"], ["AX = XB", "go where the camera sees"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Find the transform between the <strong>camera frame and the robot frame<\/strong> (camera on the arm = eye-in-hand; fixed camera = eye-to-hand).<\/li>\n<li>Solved from several robot poses viewing a target (<strong>AX = XB<\/strong>, cv2.calibrateHandEye), so the robot can <strong>go where the camera sees<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I07", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Domain shift: works in the lab, fails on site", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["lighting, dust, angle and wear", "collect site data early", "Monitor"], "hint2": [["lighting, dust, angle and wear"], ["collect site data early", "brightness, exposure, blur", "per condition"], ["Monitor", "retrain"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>On site the <strong>lighting, dust, angle and wear<\/strong> differ from the training data.<\/li>\n<li>Fix: <strong>collect site data early<\/strong>; augment <strong>brightness, exposure, blur<\/strong>; test <strong>per condition<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Monitor<\/strong> confidence and drift; <strong>retrain<\/strong> on logged images.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I08", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Acceptance testing, shadow mode, commissioning", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["FAT", "Shadow mode", "Commissioning"], "hint2": [["FAT", "SAT"], ["Shadow mode", "logs decisions without acting"], ["Commissioning", "acceptance criteria set beforehand"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>FAT<\/strong> = factory acceptance test (in the shop); <strong>SAT<\/strong> = site acceptance test (after installation).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shadow mode:<\/strong> the system runs and <strong>logs decisions without acting<\/strong>, compared against the operators.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Commissioning:<\/strong> install, tune, and prove it meets <strong>acceptance criteria set beforehand<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I09", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Harsh environments", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Dust and steam", "Heat", "Vibration and changing light"], "hint2": [["Dust and steam", "sealed (IP-rated) enclosures", "air knives or purge air"], ["Heat", "standoff distance", "drifts with temperature"], ["Vibration and changing light", "your own lighting"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Dust and steam:<\/strong> <strong>sealed (IP-rated) enclosures<\/strong>, <strong>air knives or purge air<\/strong>, a cleaning schedule.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Heat:<\/strong> cooling jackets, <strong>standoff distance<\/strong>, calibration <strong>drifts with temperature<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Vibration and changing light:<\/strong> rigid mounts, <strong>your own lighting<\/strong>, fixed exposure, regular calibration checks.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "I10", "sec": "I", "tag": "Industrial vision", "prompt": "Annotation and synchronisation", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Annotation", "Sync sensors", "raw data + metadata"], "hint2": [["Annotation", "labelling guidelines", "CVAT", "double-check a sample"], ["Sync sensors", "hardware trigger", "PTP / IEEE 1588", "timestamp everything"], ["raw data + metadata"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Annotation:<\/strong> clear <strong>labelling guidelines<\/strong>, a tool (<strong>CVAT<\/strong>, Label Studio), and <strong>double-check a sample<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sync sensors:<\/strong> <strong>hardware trigger<\/strong> or a shared clock (<strong>PTP / IEEE 1588<\/strong>); <strong>timestamp everything<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Keep <strong>raw data + metadata<\/strong> (exposure, time, camera) so results can be traced.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N01", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Convolution", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["small kernel", "what it finds", "same kernel everywhere"], "hint2": [["small kernel", "multiply the overlapping values and add them up"], ["what it finds", "CNN the kernel numbers are learned"], ["same kernel everywhere", "few parameters", "anywhere"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Slide a <strong>small kernel<\/strong> (e.g. 3×3 numbers) over the image; at each spot <strong>multiply the overlapping values and add them up<\/strong> → one output pixel.<\/li>\n<li>The kernel’s numbers decide <strong>what it finds<\/strong> (an edge, a blur). In a <strong>CNN the kernel numbers are learned<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>same kernel everywhere<\/strong>: <strong>few parameters<\/strong>, and it finds the pattern <strong>anywhere<\/strong> in the image.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><em>Simply:<\/em> a stencil you slide across the picture. The output lights up wherever the picture matches the stencil.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/convolution.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "N02", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "ReLU and pooling", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["ReLU", "Max pooling", "Adaptive average pooling"], "hint2": [["ReLU", "negatives become 0"], ["Max pooling", "largest value", "half the size"], ["Adaptive average pooling", "fixed grid"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>ReLU:<\/strong> max(0, x); <strong>negatives become 0<\/strong>. This adds the non-linearity a network needs.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Max pooling:<\/strong> keep the <strong>largest value<\/strong> in each 2×2 window, so the image is <strong>half the size<\/strong> but keeps the strongest responses.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Adaptive average pooling:<\/strong> resize to a <strong>fixed grid<\/strong> (Luminance-GS: 16 tokens).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/relu_pool.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "N03", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Linear layer", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["y = Wx + b", "Linear 128 → 255"], "hint2": [["y = Wx + b", "matrix multiply"], ["Linear 128 → 255"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>y = Wx + b<\/strong>: a learned <strong>matrix multiply<\/strong> plus a bias; every input connects to every output.<\/li>\n<li>In Luminance-GS a final <strong>Linear 128 → 255<\/strong> outputs the per-view curve bias.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N04", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Attention", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["query", "Softmax", "weighted average of the values"], "hint2": [["query", "key", "scores"], ["Softmax", "weights that sum to 1"], ["weighted average of the values"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A <strong>query<\/strong> is compared with each <strong>key<\/strong> (a dot product), giving similarity <strong>scores<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Softmax<\/strong> turns the scores into <strong>weights that sum to 1<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The output is the <strong>weighted average of the values<\/strong>: “look mostly where the query matches”.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/attention.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "N05", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Loss, gradient, backpropagation", "n": 3, "star": false, "hint1": ["Loss", "Gradient", "Backpropagation"], "hint2": [["Loss", "how wrong"], ["Gradient", "slope"], ["Backpropagation", "chain rule", "backwards", "every parameter"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Loss:<\/strong> one number for <strong>how wrong<\/strong> the model is; training makes it smaller.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gradient:<\/strong> the <strong>slope<\/strong> of the loss for each parameter: which way to move it, and how hard.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Backpropagation:<\/strong> the <strong>chain rule<\/strong> applied <strong>backwards<\/strong> from the loss through every layer, giving the gradient for <strong>every parameter<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/gradient.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "N06", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Optimiser vocabulary", "n": 4, "star": false, "hint1": ["Learning rate", "Adam", "Weight decay", "Schedule"], "hint2": [["Learning rate"], ["Adam", "running averages", "each parameter gets its own step size"], ["Weight decay", "regularisation"], ["Schedule", "lower the learning rate", "1%"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Learning rate:<\/strong> the step size (too big → bounces or diverges; too small → slow).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Adam:<\/strong> keeps <strong>running averages<\/strong> of each gradient and its square, so <strong>each parameter gets its own step size<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Weight decay:<\/strong> pulls weights toward 0, a form of <strong>regularisation<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Schedule:<\/strong> <strong>lower the learning rate<\/strong> over training (Luminance-GS decays it to <strong>1%<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N07", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Overfitting", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["memorises the training data", "more or augmented data", "held-out"], "hint2": [["memorises the training data", "training loss falls, validation loss rises"], ["more or augmented data", "regularisation", "early stopping"], ["held-out"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>The model <strong>memorises the training data<\/strong> and fails on new data: <strong>training loss falls, validation loss rises<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Fixes: <strong>more or augmented data<\/strong>, <strong>regularisation<\/strong> (weight decay, dropout), <strong>early stopping<\/strong>, a smaller model.<\/li>\n<li>Always judge on a <strong>held-out<\/strong> set.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N08", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Train / validation / test, and data leakage", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["Train", "Leakage", "split by session or day"], "hint2": [["Train", "validation", "test", "once"], ["Leakage", "too good"], ["split by session or day"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Train<\/strong> fits the weights; <strong>validation<\/strong> guides choices; <strong>test<\/strong> is used <strong>once<\/strong>, at the end.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Leakage:<\/strong> test information sneaks into training, so results look <strong>too good<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>For video or conveyor frames, <strong>split by session or day<\/strong>, not by random frame (neighbouring frames are nearly identical).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N09", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "Transfer learning and augmentation", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["Transfer learning", "Augmentation"], "hint2": [["Transfer learning", "pretrained weights", "fine-tune"], ["Augmentation", "brightness and exposure", "site conditions"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>Transfer learning:<\/strong> start from <strong>pretrained weights<\/strong> (e.g. COCO) and <strong>fine-tune<\/strong> on a few hundred of your own images.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Augmentation:<\/strong> flips, crops, <strong>brightness and exposure<\/strong>, blur, noise, so the model copes with <strong>site conditions<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N10", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "CNN (convolutional neural network)", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["small learned filters", "Hierarchy", "Why for images"], "hint2": [["small learned filters", "ReLU", "downsampling"], ["Hierarchy", "edges → textures → parts → objects", "head", "classes or boxes"], ["Why for images", "same filter everywhere", "far fewer weights", "anywhere", "Darknet-53", "53-layer"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>Layers of <strong>small learned filters<\/strong> that slide over the image (card N01), each followed by <strong>ReLU<\/strong>, with <strong>downsampling<\/strong> between stages.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hierarchy:<\/strong> <strong>edges → textures → parts → objects<\/strong>; a <strong>head<\/strong> turns the last maps into <strong>classes or boxes<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why for images:<\/strong> the <strong>same filter everywhere<\/strong> (weight sharing) means <strong>far fewer weights<\/strong>, and it finds a pattern <strong>anywhere<\/strong>. YOLOv3’s backbone, <strong>Darknet-53<\/strong>, is a <strong>53-layer<\/strong> CNN.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/cnn.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}, {"id": "N11", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "DNN (deep neural network)", "n": 2, "star": false, "hint1": ["many layers", "OpenCV’s DNN module"], "hint2": [["many layers", "CNNs", "transformers"], ["OpenCV’s DNN module", "runs", "can’t train", "trained in Darknet", "ran in OpenCV"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li>A neural network with <strong>many layers<\/strong> between input and output. <strong>CNNs<\/strong> and <strong>transformers<\/strong> are kinds of DNN.<\/li>\n<li><strong>OpenCV’s DNN module<\/strong> only <strong>runs<\/strong> networks trained elsewhere (Darknet, ONNX, TensorFlow); it <strong>can’t train<\/strong>. We <strong>trained in Darknet<\/strong> and <strong>ran in OpenCV<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n"}, {"id": "N12", "sec": "N", "tag": "ML fundamentals", "prompt": "CUDA, and why vision uses GPUs", "n": 3, "star": true, "hint1": ["CUDA", "Why vision", "Mine"], "hint2": [["CUDA", "general code on the GPU", "cuDNN", "neural-network"], ["Why vision", "millions of pixels", "same multiply-add at every pixel", "thousands of GPU cores"], ["Mine", "OpenCV DNN on CUDA", "PyTorch", "gsplat’s CUDA renderer", "RTX 3080 Ti", "5 to 8 min"]], "answer": "<ul>\n<li><strong>CUDA<\/strong> = NVIDIA’s platform for running <strong>general code on the GPU<\/strong>; <strong>cuDNN<\/strong> = its library of fast <strong>neural-network<\/strong> operations.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Why vision:<\/strong> images are <strong>millions of pixels<\/strong>, and convolution does the <strong>same multiply-add at every pixel<\/strong>, so the work splits across <strong>thousands of GPU cores<\/strong> at once.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mine:<\/strong> capstone, <strong>OpenCV DNN on CUDA<\/strong> (~0.2 → 0.09 s); research, <strong>PyTorch<\/strong> + <strong>gsplat’s CUDA renderer<\/strong> on an <strong>RTX 3080 Ti<\/strong>, about <strong>5 to 8 min<\/strong> per training run.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/cpu_gpu.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n"}], "sheets": [{"title": "Sheet 1 · Research project: low-light 3D reconstruction", "html": "<p><strong>One line:<\/strong> I showed that a 255,000-parameter brightening module inside a CVPR 2025 3D reconstruction method was really doing something simple, replaced it with a 70-parameter physical model, and tested it with rules fixed in advance.<\/p>\n<h2>What<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Base method:<\/strong> Luminance-GS (CVPR 2025) builds a <strong>3D Gaussian Splatting<\/strong> scene from <strong>dark photos<\/strong> that renders <strong>normally lit<\/strong>, with no normal-light training images.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Its brightening part:<\/strong> a learned <strong>tone curve per photo<\/strong> (255-entry lookup table) from <strong>two neural networks<\/strong> + a colour transform: about <strong>255k parameters<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>My replacement:<\/strong> <strong>one shared monotone curve F + one exposure s_k per photo<\/strong>: L_k(x) = F(min(1, 2^{s_k} x)). <strong>55 to 76 parameters<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/lgs_pipeline.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<h2>Why<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Research question:<\/strong> does the big module reduce to a simple, explicit model, and is that model <strong>as good on normal data<\/strong> and <strong>better when exposure varies<\/strong>?<\/li>\n<li><strong>Industrial angle:<\/strong> cameras in the field <strong>disagree on exposure<\/strong> (auto-exposure, several cameras, changing light). A model that <strong>separates the scene from the camera<\/strong> should hold up where a black box doesn’t.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>How<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Reproduce<\/strong> (9 runs): same seed differs by up to <strong>1 dB<\/strong>, so <strong>3 seeds<\/strong> per setting and effects count only above <strong>2× seed noise<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Diagnose before building<\/strong> (Rung 0, 30 runs): curves already <strong>monotone<\/strong>; views differ by <strong>one number<\/strong> that tracks brightness (<strong>over 99%<\/strong>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Untangle a confound<\/strong> (Rung 0.5, 2×2, 60 runs): the “detach” gain is <strong>85 to 100%<\/strong> from the curve, not the colour transform. “Detach” becomes the <strong>baseline to beat<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Build a test with a known answer<\/strong> (M1): each training photo brightened or darkened by a <strong>known gain, up to ±1 stop<\/strong>. The original’s per-view curves go the <strong>wrong way<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Build and test Rung 2<\/strong> (65 runs), with <strong>H1 and H2 rules written before any run<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/tone_design.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<h2>Results<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Test<\/th>\n<th>Rung 2 vs <code>detach<\/code><\/th>\n<th>Verdict<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>H2:<\/strong> varying exposure<\/td>\n<td><strong>+2.11 dB<\/strong> brightness-matched PSNR pooled (<strong>5.3× noise<\/strong>); ahead in 4 of 4 decision scenes; LPIPS better<\/td>\n<td><strong>Supported<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Exposure leak (0 = corrected, 1 = passes through)<\/td>\n<td><strong>0.01<\/strong> vs 0.51 (official 0.41)<\/td>\n<td>Mechanism confirmed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Repeatability, varying set<\/td>\n<td>seed spread <strong>0.11 dB<\/strong> vs 0.72 dB<\/td>\n<td>Far more repeatable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>H1:<\/strong> even exposure (LOM)<\/td>\n<td>pooled <strong>−0.02 dB<\/strong>; <strong>11 of 12<\/strong> checks pass; <strong>bike −0.155 dB<\/strong> vs a −0.082 dB bar<\/td>\n<td><strong>Not supported<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost<\/td>\n<td><strong>55 to 76<\/strong> vs ~255,000 curve parameters; <strong>4.6 vs 7.8 min<\/strong> per run<\/td>\n<td>~40% faster<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/results_slope.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/leak.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<p><strong>How to read the leak figure:<\/strong> x = the gain I applied to a training photo; y = how much of that gain reached the photo’s pseudo-target. Dashed (slope 1) = passes straight through; flat (slope 0) = corrected. Official and detach let <strong>about half<\/strong> through; Rung 2 is <strong>flat<\/strong>. That is the mechanism behind the +2.11 dB.<\/p>\n<h2>What it means<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The big module’s per-photo behaviour <strong>is essentially one exposure number<\/strong>, and modelling it explicitly <strong>fixes what the networks got wrong<\/strong> (the leak).<\/li>\n<li>When exposure varies, the simple model is <strong>clearly better and more stable<\/strong>. On even lighting it <strong>matches overall<\/strong> but narrowly <strong>fails one strict per-scene check<\/strong>, which I report as a miss.<\/li>\n<li>A pre-declared follow-up showed the result depends on <strong>where the curve starts<\/strong> (+0.3 dB on one seed, exploratory): the next lever.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>Limits and next<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>5 scenes, 3 seeds<\/strong>; exposure changes <strong>synthetic<\/strong>; <strong>hue<\/strong> worse on bike; F learns from <strong>2D histograms only<\/strong> by default.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Next:<\/strong> H3 (let only the exposures learn from the render), a colour-preserving variant, the curve’s dark end. <strong>Freeze 5 Dec, report 18 Dec.<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>Numbers to have ready<\/h2>\n<p><strong>255k → 55 to 76<\/strong> parameters · <strong>+2.11 dB<\/strong> (5.3× noise) · leak <strong>0.01 vs 0.51<\/strong> · <strong>23.23 → 23.11<\/strong> vs <strong>23.25 → 21.03<\/strong> · <strong>11 of 12<\/strong> checks, bike <strong>−0.155 dB<\/strong> · <strong>~160<\/strong> training runs · <strong>40%<\/strong> faster · <strong>3 seeds, 2× noise rule<\/strong><\/p>\n"}, {"title": "Sheet 2 · Capstone: the CCPU, a vision-guided cup-sorting robot", "html": "<p><strong>One line:<\/strong> Our team of three built a robot that spots Tim Hortons cups in mixed recycling on a moving conveyor and lifts them out with a vacuum gantry timed to the belt: 95% removed, under 1% false positives in about 100 trials.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_rig.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\">\n<img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_system.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<h2>What<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>conveyor<\/strong> carries mixed recycling; a <strong>webcam ~1 m above<\/strong> watches it; an <strong>x–z gantry<\/strong> with a <strong>suction cup<\/strong> downstream lifts <strong>Tim Hortons cups<\/strong> into a <strong>low-grade compost<\/strong> bin.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sept 2022 to Apr 2023<\/strong>, ENGPHYS 4A06, team of <strong>3<\/strong>: Dayyan (mechanical, power), Shahaan (vision lead), me (<strong>team lead, gantry design, motor control code, integration<\/strong>, and much of the vision work). Shown at the <strong>Capstone Expo<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>Why<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Single-use cups <strong>aren’t recyclable<\/strong> (plastic liner). Our report cited <strong>over 1 million a day<\/strong> thrown out in the GTA. Hamilton uses them as <strong>low-grade compost<\/strong>, but <strong>sorts by hand<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Tim Hortons cups<\/strong> are the most common, and a clear first target: <strong>red, distinctive shape<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The engineering problem: <strong>see, decide and act on a moving target<\/strong>, on time.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>How<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Capture:<\/strong> <strong>LED strips<\/strong> along both sides of the belt; 960×720 at <strong>short exposure + high gain<\/strong> to stop motion blur; autofocus off.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Preprocess:<\/strong> resize to <strong>416×416<\/strong>, ÷255, BGR → RGB.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Detect:<\/strong> <strong>YOLOv3<\/strong>, one class, in <strong>OpenCV DNN on CUDA<\/strong>: ~<strong>0.09 s<\/strong> per frame (CPU ~0.2 s).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Filter:<\/strong> confidence &gt; <strong>0.5<\/strong>, then <strong>NMS<\/strong> at IoU 0.4.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Locate:<\/strong> box centre → cm on the belt (~<strong>0.7 mm per pixel<\/strong>); each cup reported <strong>once<\/strong>, from a <strong>7 cm zone<\/strong>; queued and moved along at <strong>belt speed<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Send:<\/strong> the cup’s <strong>lane (0 to 17 in)<\/strong> over <strong>USB serial<\/strong> to the gantry <strong>ESP32<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Pick (my code):<\/strong> inches → steps (<strong>127 steps per inch<\/strong>); move across; <strong>wait = 2.3 s − arm time − 0.55 s<\/strong>; lower, <strong>valve closes<\/strong> (suction), lift, carry to the bin, <strong>valve opens<\/strong> (drop), return, send <strong>“done”<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>No-skip mode (mine):<\/strong> if too many cups come, the program <strong>freezes the belt<\/strong> until the arm is free, so <strong>no cup is missed<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_timing.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<p><strong>Training the detector<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>912 images<\/strong> from our own webcam on the real belt: cups (new, crushed, lid on or off) <strong>mixed with random recycling<\/strong>, about 10% with <strong>no cups<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Label Studio<\/strong> on an <strong>AWS<\/strong> server (all three labelling at once); <strong>Darknet on Google Colab<\/strong> from a <strong>pretrained Darknet-53<\/strong> backbone; 2,000 iterations, about 3 h; 10% held out.<\/li>\n<li>Three versions: <strong>179 → 311 → 912<\/strong> images. Each added the <strong>things it confused<\/strong> with cups.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/capstone_before_after.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<h2>Results<\/h2>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure<\/th>\n<th>Value<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cups detected and removed<\/td>\n<td><strong>95%<\/strong> (about 100 manual checklist trials)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>False positives<\/td>\n<td><strong>under 1%<\/strong>; final model: none observed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Detection time<\/td>\n<td><strong>~0.2 s CPU → 0.09 s GPU<\/strong> (about 2.3×)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pick timing<\/td>\n<td>cup under the arm <strong>~2.3 s<\/strong> after detection; full cycle <strong>~8 s<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Main failure<\/td>\n<td><strong>no seal<\/strong> on crumpled cups or the rim<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget<\/td>\n<td>about <strong>$590<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2>Safety<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Instant-stop switch<\/strong> and an easy-to-reach <strong>power cord<\/strong>; <strong>temperature sensors<\/strong> on the electronics (fans to max when hot, a <strong>warning<\/strong> if too hot); <strong>halt on a jam<\/strong>; <strong>side barriers<\/strong> on the belt; <strong>start-up and running warning lights<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>What it means<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li>A <strong>full vision-to-actuator loop<\/strong>: data, model, latency, timing and mechanics all had to work <strong>together<\/strong>. A <strong>simple interface<\/strong> (one number over serial) made integration easy.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Hard negatives matter:<\/strong> the model learned <strong>“red ≠ cup”<\/strong> only after we showed it red cans and cartons.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The arm, not the detector, set the throughput<\/strong> (an 8 s cycle). Same lesson as Hatch’s <strong>“on the fly”<\/strong> robotics: <strong>sync motion with a moving process<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>What I would do differently now<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Metrics from day one:<\/strong> precision, recall and mAP on a held-out set from <strong>another day<\/strong>. Our report admits we judged by eye.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Encoder<\/strong> on the belt instead of an assumed speed; a <strong>hardware trigger<\/strong> for the camera.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Controlled lighting<\/strong> in a hood; fixed exposure.<\/li>\n<li><strong>A newer YOLO → ONNX / TensorRT on a Jetson<\/strong>; log every pick.<\/li>\n<li><strong>PLC, e-stop and guarding<\/strong> instead of microcontrollers.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<h2>Numbers to have ready<\/h2>\n<p><strong>912<\/strong> images · <strong>95%<\/strong> removed · <strong>under 1%<\/strong> false positives · <strong>~100<\/strong> trials · <strong>0.2 → 0.09 s<\/strong> on GPU · <strong>416×416<\/strong> input · confidence <strong>0.5<\/strong>, NMS <strong>0.4<\/strong> · belt <strong>22.5 cm/s<\/strong> · pick at <strong>~2.3 s<\/strong> · <strong>127 steps per inch<\/strong> · pump <strong>−65 kPa<\/strong> · team of <strong>3<\/strong> · <strong>$590<\/strong><\/p>\n"}, {"title": "Sheet 3 · Designing a vision system: the method, and a mining example", "html": "<p><strong>Use it when they ask:<\/strong> “How would you design a vision system for X?” Ask two or three questions first, walk the eight steps, then say how you would prove it works.<\/p>\n<h2>The method in 8 steps<\/h2>\n<p><em>Mnemonic:<\/em> <strong>R<\/strong>eally <strong>S<\/strong>mart <strong>L<\/strong>ights <strong>D<\/strong>on’t <strong>M<\/strong>ake <strong>I<\/strong>ndustrial <strong>V<\/strong>ision <strong>M<\/strong>agic.<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step<\/th>\n<th>What to decide<\/th>\n<th>A line to say<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. <strong>Requirements<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>What must be seen, what decision follows, smallest feature, line speed, latency, <strong>cost of a miss vs a false alarm<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>“First I’d pin down what a miss costs compared with a false alarm.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. <strong>Sensor<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>2D, 3D, thermal or line-scan; pixel size = field of view ÷ pixels, with <strong>3 to 5 px<\/strong> across the smallest feature; <strong>global shutter<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>“Exposure must be short enough that the part moves less than a pixel.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. <strong>Lighting + protection<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Backlight, dark-field, diffuse or structured light; <strong>strobe<\/strong>; <strong>hood<\/strong>; sealed enclosure, heating or cooling, <strong>air knife<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>“Control the light and you’ve solved half the problem.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4. <strong>Data<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Collect <strong>on site, early<\/strong>, in every condition; label; a <strong>test set from other days<\/strong>; <strong>seeded<\/strong> known defects<\/td>\n<td>“I’d collect on site before choosing a model.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5. <strong>Model<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Classical first<\/strong> (thresholds, blobs, edges, 3D geometry), then ML (detector, segmentation, anomaly detection); fits the <strong>edge latency<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>“Start with the simplest thing that could work, and measure it.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6. <strong>Integrate<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Trigger (<strong>encoder<\/strong> or photo-eye); <strong>calibration<\/strong> (camera → world → robot); <strong>PLC handshake<\/strong>; <strong>fail-safe<\/strong> default; timing budget<\/td>\n<td>“The PLC decides; vision gives it a clear, timed answer.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7. <strong>Validate<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Pass/fail rules <strong>written first<\/strong>; precision and recall <strong>per condition<\/strong>; lab → <strong>shadow mode<\/strong> → commissioning<\/td>\n<td>“Same discipline as my research: rules before results.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8. <strong>Maintain<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Monitoring, <strong>drift<\/strong>, cleaning, retraining, documentation and handover<\/td>\n<td>“A vision system is never finished at commissioning.”<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<p><strong>Two numbers worth knowing:<\/strong> max exposure = pixel size ÷ speed (1 mm pixels at 4 m/s → <strong>0.25 ms<\/strong>). Time budget = distance to the action point ÷ speed (20 m at 4 m/s → <strong>5 s<\/strong>).<\/p>\n<h2>Worked example: oversize rocks and tramp metal on a crusher feed belt<\/h2>\n<p><strong>Why this one:<\/strong> it’s my capstone at mine scale: detect on a moving belt, then act in time. Tramp metal (bolts, shovel teeth, rebar) and oversize rocks <strong>damage crushers and block chutes<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<p><img class=\"fig\" src=\"figs/mining_conveyor.png\" alt=\"figure\" loading=\"lazy\"><\/p>\n<p><strong>Questions I’d ask first:<\/strong> belt speed and width? Which objects, and the smallest size? What action (stop, divert, robot pick, alarm), and how far before the crusher? What does a false stop cost?<\/p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What I’d account for<\/th>\n<th>How I’d deal with it<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Dust and mud<\/strong> on the window<\/td>\n<td>Sealed hood, <strong>air knife<\/strong> or purge, a wiper; a <strong>dirty-lens self-check<\/strong> (sharpness drops → alarm); a cleaning schedule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Sun, shadows, night<\/strong>, or dark underground<\/td>\n<td>Hood to block outside light; <strong>own LED light, strobed<\/strong> with the exposure; exposure normalisation (my research)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Fast belt<\/strong> (2 to 6 m/s) → blur<\/td>\n<td><strong>Global shutter<\/strong> or line-scan; exposure ≤ pixel ÷ speed; strobe; <strong>encoder trigger<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Everything is grey and dusty<\/strong>, so colour is useless<\/td>\n<td><strong>3D laser line profiler<\/strong>: height and size directly; a <strong>metal detector<\/strong> for steel; combine both<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rocks overlap and pile up<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>3D segmentation on the height map; measure the visible top; tune thresholds with operations<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Rare events<\/strong>, few examples<\/td>\n<td><strong>Anomaly detection<\/strong> on normal ore; synthetic insertion; <strong>seeded tests<\/strong> with real objects; human review of flags<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Vibration, heat, cold<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Isolated mounts; rated housings with heating or cooling; calibration check against a <strong>fixed target<\/strong> in view<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Time to act<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Budget = distance ÷ belt speed; <strong>edge PC on site<\/strong>, no cloud<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Integration and safety<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>PLC handshake<\/strong> (ready, result, acknowledge) with a heartbeat; <strong>fail-safe<\/strong> if the camera dies (alarm, fall back to the metal detector); vision is <strong>never a safety device<\/strong> for people; lock-out before entering the cell<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Proving it works<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>Shadow mode<\/strong> for weeks; <strong>seeded objects<\/strong> for recall; <strong>false stops per shift<\/strong>; acceptance rules written <strong>before<\/strong> commissioning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Ore changes over time<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Watch confidence and false-stop rates; re-label and retrain; version the models<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table>\n<h2>If the example is a smelter instead<\/h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Glare and radiant heat<\/strong> from molten metal: <strong>narrow-band or near-infrared filters<\/strong>, ND filters, <strong>HDR<\/strong>, or a <strong>thermal camera<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Fumes and steam:<\/strong> longer wavelengths (<strong>thermal<\/strong>) see through better; air purge on the window.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Heat on the hardware:<\/strong> <strong>water- or air-cooled<\/strong> housings, distance, heat shields.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Typical robot tasks<\/strong> (industry examples, not confirmed Hatch projects): <strong>tapping and plugging furnaces<\/strong>, <strong>sampling<\/strong>, <strong>anode handling<\/strong>. Vision <strong>locates the target<\/strong> for the robot; <strong>telerobotics<\/strong> keeps people back.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h2>How to say it in the room (2 minutes)<\/h2>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ask two or three questions<\/strong> (speed, smallest object, cost of each kind of error).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Walk the 8 steps<\/strong>, one sentence each, with the example’s specifics.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Name the trade-off<\/strong> (false stops vs misses) and who decides it (operations, with the client).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Close with validation:<\/strong> “rules written before testing, shadow mode, then commissioning”, and link it to my research (rules fixed before any run) and my capstone (a moving belt, timed picks).<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n"}]};
